(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Database Systems"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Database Systems"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Database Systems: Assignments\n\nThree assignments, seven questions each (21 in all), with complete solutions at the end of each assignment. Questions in Assignment 1 use the university registry defined in the lecture notes (student, course, enrol); you can recreate it in SQLite or PostgreSQL and run every query yourself. Always state assumptions and show how you derived the answers.\n\n# Assignment 1: Relational Algebra and SQL (Chapters 1 to 3)\n\n**Q1. Relational algebra.** Using the registry, write algebra expressions and give the results for: (a) the names of CE students in year 2 or later [3]; (b) the names of students who took CE202 but not CE201 [3]; (c) the sids of students who took both CE201 and CE202 [2]; (d) the cardinality of student × course and of student ⋈ enrol [2].\n\n**Q2. Reading SQL.** Give the exact result of each query: (a) `SELECT dept, COUNT(*) FROM student GROUP BY dept ORDER BY dept;` [2] (b) `SELECT DISTINCT name FROM student JOIN enrol USING (sid) WHERE dept = 'CE' AND grade >= 70 ORDER BY name;` [3] (c) `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM student s LEFT JOIN enrol e ON s.sid = e.sid AND e.cid = 'CE202';` and the same with the condition moved to WHERE [3] (d) `SELECT cid, COUNT(*) FROM enrol WHERE grade >= 60 GROUP BY cid HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2 ORDER BY cid;` [2]\n\n**Q3. Joins and aggregates.** Write and evaluate queries for: (a) every student with their number of enrolments, including those with none, ordered by count descending then name [3]; (b) for each course, the number of enrolments, the average and the top grade, including empty courses [3]; (c) the names and total credits of courses passed (grade at least 60) by each student, largest total first [4].\n\n**Q4. Subqueries and NULL.** (a) Names of students who did not take CE202, using NOT EXISTS [3]. (b) Names of students with at least three enrolments, using a scalar subquery [2]. (c) Courses in which nobody scored below 60 [2]. (d) Let t = {1, 2, NULL} and u = {2, NULL}. Evaluate `SELECT a FROM t WHERE a NOT IN (SELECT a FROM u)` and the NOT EXISTS version and explain [3].\n\n**Q5. Schema definition.** A library holds books, members and loans. A book has an ISBN, title and a number of copies; a member has an id, name and email (unique); a loan records which member borrowed which book on which date and when it was returned (NULL if not yet returned). (a) Write CREATE TABLE statements with keys, foreign keys and constraints [6]. (b) Write a query listing members with overdue loans (borrowed more than 14 days before 2025-06-30 and not returned) [2]. (c) Write an index you would create and explain why [2].\n\n**Q6. Window functions and CASE.** (a) For course MA101 list each student's grade with its rank, highest first [3]. (b) For MA101 show each grade's deviation from the course average [3]. (c) Convert the CE201 grades into letters (A at least 80, B at least 70, C at least 60, otherwise F) [2]. (d) Which pairs of students share CE201? [2]\n\n**Q7. Programming with SQL.** (a) Write a Python function using sqlite3 that returns the average grade of a given student, using a parameterised query [4]. (b) Show how string concatenation lets an attacker obtain all students, and why placeholders prevent it [3]. (c) Write a transaction that moves a student from CE201 to CE202 keeping their grade of 78 and explain what happens if the second statement fails [3].\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 1\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) π_name(σ_{dept='CE' ∧ year>=2}(student)) = **{Ama, Kofi, Yaw, Nii}** (years 2, 3, 2, 3).\n(b) π_name(student ⋈ σ_{cid='CE202'}(enrol)) - π_name(student ⋈ σ_{cid='CE201'}(enrol)): CE202 has {Ama, Yaw, Nii}, CE201 has {Ama, Kofi, Yaw}, so the difference is **{Nii}**. (Expressed on sids: π_sid(σ_{cid='CE202'}) - π_sid(σ_{cid='CE201'}) = {8}.)\n(c) π_sid(σ_{cid='CE201'}(enrol)) ∩ π_sid(σ_{cid='CE202'}(enrol)) = {1, 2, 4} ∩ {1, 4, 8} = **{1, 4}**.\n(d) |student × course| = 8 × 6 = **48**; student ⋈ enrol has one tuple per enrolment: **16**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Step 1: group the 8 students by dept: CE = {Ama, Kofi, Yaw, Akua, Nii} (5), EE = {Esi, Kwame} (2), ME = {Abena} (1).\nStep 2: ORDER BY dept gives (CE, 5), (EE, 2), (ME, 1).\n(b) Step 1: join student and enrol, keep dept = 'CE' and grade >= 70: Ama CE201 78, Ama CE202 85, Ama MA101 70, Kofi CE301 72, Nii CE301 77, Akua MA101 88 (Kofi's CE201 64 and Yaw's grades 55, 61, 49 fail the test).\nStep 2: DISTINCT name, ordered: **Akua, Ama, Kofi, Nii**.\n(c) With the condition in the ON clause the left join keeps every student: **8** rows (Ama, Yaw and Nii get their CE202 row, the other five get NULLs). Moving the condition to WHERE discards the NULL rows: **3** rows. This shows the difference between ON and WHERE in outer joins.\n(d) Step 1: keep enrolments with grade >= 60 (11 of the 16 rows) and count per course: CE201: 78, 64 → 2; CE202: 85, 61, 69 → 3; CE301: 72, 77 → 2; EE210: 90, 81 → 2; MA101: 70, 66, 74, 88 → 4; ME120: none.\nStep 2: HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2 keeps all of these: (CE201, 2), (CE202, 3), (CE301, 2), (EE210, 2), (MA101, 4).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a)\n```sql\nSELECT s.name, COUNT(e.cid) AS n\nFROM student s LEFT JOIN enrol e ON e.sid = s.sid\nGROUP BY s.sid ORDER BY n DESC, s.name;\n```\nStep 1: enrolments per student: Ama 3 (CE201, CE202, MA101), Yaw 3 (CE201, CE202, MA101), Kofi 2, Esi 2, Abena 2, Nii 2, Akua 1, Kwame 1 (the left join keeps students with none, but every student has at least one).\nStep 2: ORDER BY n DESC, name: Ama 3, Yaw 3, Abena 2, Esi 2, Kofi 2, Nii 2, Akua 1, Kwame 1.\n(b)\n```sql\nSELECT c.cid, COUNT(e.sid) AS n, ROUND(AVG(e.grade),1) AS avg_g, MAX(e.grade) AS top\nFROM course c LEFT JOIN enrol e ON e.cid = c.cid GROUP BY c.cid ORDER BY c.cid;\n```\nStep 1: sum and count per course: CE201: 78 + 64 + 55 = 197 over 3; CE202: 85 + 61 + 69 = 215 over 3; CE301: 72 + 77 = 149 over 2; EE210: 90 + 81 = 171 over 2; MA101: 70 + 66 + 49 + 74 + 88 = 347 over 5; ME120: 58 over 1.\nStep 2: averages: 197/3 = 65.7; 215/3 = 71.7; 149/2 = 74.5; 171/2 = 85.5; 347/5 = 69.4; 58/1 = 58.0.\nResult: CE201 (3, 65.7, 78), CE202 (3, 71.7, 85), CE301 (2, 74.5, 77), EE210 (2, 85.5, 90), MA101 (5, 69.4, 88), ME120 (1, 58.0, 58).\n(c)\n```sql\nSELECT s.name, SUM(c.credits) AS credits\nFROM student s JOIN enrol e ON s.sid = e.sid JOIN course c ON c.cid = e.cid\nWHERE e.grade >= 60 GROUP BY s.sid ORDER BY credits DESC, s.name;\n```\nStep 1: passed enrolments (grade ≥ 60) with credits: Ama CE201 (3) + CE202 (3) + MA101 (4) = 10; Esi EE210 (4) + MA101 (4) = 8; Kofi CE201 (3) + CE301 (3) = 6; Nii CE202 (3) + CE301 (3) = 6; Abena MA101 (4) = 4; Akua MA101 (4) = 4; Kwame EE210 (4) = 4; Yaw CE202 (3) = 3 (his CE201 grade 55 and MA101 grade 49 fail; he also has CE202 with 61).\nStep 2: ORDER BY credits DESC, name.\nResult: Ama 10, Esi 8, Kofi 6, Nii 6, Abena 4, Akua 4, Kwame 4, Yaw 3.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a)\n```sql\nSELECT name FROM student s\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enrol e WHERE e.sid = s.sid AND e.cid = 'CE202');\n```\nStep 1: students with a CE202 enrolment: Ama (1), Yaw (4), Nii (8).\nStep 2: NOT EXISTS keeps the other five. Result: Kofi, Esi, Abena, Kwame, Akua.\n(b)\n```sql\nSELECT s.name FROM student s\nWHERE (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM enrol e WHERE e.sid = s.sid) >= 3;\n```\nStep 1: counts of enrolments: Ama 3, Kofi 2, Esi 2, Yaw 3, Abena 2, Kwame 1, Akua 1, Nii 2.\nStep 2: keep count >= 3. Result: Ama, Yaw.\n(c)\n```sql\nSELECT c.title FROM course c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enrol e WHERE e.cid = c.cid AND e.grade < 60);\n```\nStep 1: grades below 60 exist in CE201 (Yaw 55), MA101 (Yaw 49) and ME120 (Abena 58).\nStep 2: the other courses have no enrolment below 60: CE202 (85, 61, 69), CE301 (72, 77), EE210 (90, 81).\nResult: Databases, Circuits, Operating Systems (the order is not defined without an ORDER BY).\n(d) `NOT IN` returns **no rows**: for a = 1, `1 NOT IN (2, NULL)` is `1 <> 2 AND 1 <> NULL` = TRUE AND UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN, which is not TRUE; a = 2 is in the set; a = NULL gives UNKNOWN. `NOT EXISTS` returns **{1, NULL}** because the correlated test `u.a = t.a` finds no match for either. Use NOT EXISTS (or filter the NULLs) when the subquery can return NULL.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a)\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE book (\n  isbn   TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  title  TEXT NOT NULL,\n  copies INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (copies >= 0)\n);\nCREATE TABLE member (\n  mid   INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  name  TEXT NOT NULL,\n  email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE\n);\nCREATE TABLE loan (\n  mid       INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES member(mid),\n  isbn      TEXT    NOT NULL REFERENCES book(isbn),\n  loan_date DATE    NOT NULL,\n  return_date DATE,\n  PRIMARY KEY (mid, isbn, loan_date),\n  CHECK (return_date IS NULL OR return_date >= loan_date)\n);\n```\nThe key includes the loan date so a member can borrow the same book again later.\n(b)\n```sql\nSELECT DISTINCT m.name FROM member m JOIN loan l ON l.mid = m.mid\nWHERE l.return_date IS NULL AND l.loan_date < DATE '2025-06-30' - 14;\n```\nThis is PostgreSQL syntax (date minus an integer number of days) and was run on PostgreSQL 17. In SQLite, which stores dates as text, write `date('2025-06-30', '-14 days')` instead. Declaring the columns as DATE (not TEXT) matters: PostgreSQL rejects comparing a TEXT column with a date.\n(c) `CREATE INDEX idx_loan_open ON loan(return_date, loan_date);` (or a partial index on rows with return_date IS NULL): queries for overdue loans filter on those columns, and an index avoids scanning the whole loan table. A foreign-key index on loan(isbn) also speeds the join from book to loans.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a)\n```sql\nSELECT name, grade, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY grade DESC) AS r\nFROM student JOIN enrol USING (sid) WHERE cid = 'MA101';\n```\nResult: Akua 88 (1), Abena 74 (2), Ama 70 (3), Esi 66 (4), Yaw 49 (5).\n(b) Step 1: the average MA101 grade is (70 + 66 + 49 + 74 + 88)/5 = 347/5 = 69.4.\nStep 2: deviations: Akua 88 - 69.4 = +18.6; Abena 74 - 69.4 = +4.6; Ama 70 - 69.4 = +0.6; Esi 66 - 69.4 = -3.4; Yaw 49 - 69.4 = -20.4 (query: `grade - AVG(grade) OVER (PARTITION BY cid)`).\n(c) CE201 grades: Ama 78 ≥ 70 and < 80: B; Kofi 64 ≥ 60 and < 70: C; Yaw 55 < 60: F.\n(d) Step 1: the CE201 students are sids 1, 2 and 4 (Ama, Kofi, Yaw).\nStep 2: a self join with e1.sid < e2.sid lists each pair once: (Ama, Kofi), (Ama, Yaw), (Kofi, Yaw), i.e. sids (1, 2), (1, 4), (2, 4).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a)\n```python\nimport sqlite3\n\ndef average_grade(con, sid):\n    cur = con.execute(\"SELECT AVG(grade) FROM enrol WHERE sid = ?\", (sid,))\n    return cur.fetchone()[0]\n```\nFor sid 1 the grades are 78, 85, 70: AVG = (78 + 85 + 70)/3 = 233/3 = 77.67.\n(b) With `\"SELECT * FROM student WHERE name = '\" + name + \"'\"` and name = `x' OR '1'='1`, the statement becomes `... WHERE name = 'x' OR '1'='1'` which is true for every row, so all students are returned (and a `; DROP TABLE` style payload could do worse in systems that permit multiple statements). With a placeholder the whole string is bound as a **value**, never parsed as SQL, so it just matches nobody.\n(c)\n```sql\nBEGIN;\nDELETE FROM enrol WHERE sid = 1 AND cid = 'CE201';\nINSERT INTO enrol (sid, cid, grade) VALUES (1, 'CE202', 78);\nCOMMIT;\n```\nAma already has a CE202 enrolment (85), so the INSERT violates the primary key and fails. The application must issue ROLLBACK, which restores the deleted CE201 row (atomicity); otherwise the transaction leaves the student with no CE201 enrolment and no new one. (Choosing a student without CE202 would succeed.) Note for PostgreSQL: after any error inside a transaction block the transaction is marked aborted and every further statement is refused until the block ends; a COMMIT at that point silently performs a ROLLBACK (tested on PostgreSQL 17: the CE201 row was still present afterwards). Other systems let you continue after the error.\n\n# Assignment 2: Database Design (Chapters 2 and 4)\n\n**Q1. ER to tables.** A clinic has doctors (id, name, specialty), patients (id, name, date of birth, several phone numbers) and appointments: a patient may see many doctors and a doctor many patients, and each appointment has a date, time and diagnosis. Each doctor belongs to exactly one department (name, building) and a department has many doctors. (a) Identify the entities, relationships, cardinalities and the multivalued attribute [3]. (b) Give the relational schema with primary and foreign keys [5]. (c) Where would you record the \"head of department\"? [2]\n\n**Q2. Closures.** R(A, B, C, D, E), F = {A → B, BC → D, D → E}. (a) Compute A+, BC+, AC+ and D+ [4]. (b) Find all candidate keys and justify [3]. (c) Does AC → E follow from F? Does BC → E? [3]\n\n**Q3. Keys.** R(A, B, C, D), F = {AB → C, C → D, D → A}. Find all candidate keys and the prime attributes [10].\n\n**Q4. Normal forms.** State the highest normal form (1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF) of each relation and justify: (a) R(A, B, C), F = {A → B, B → C} [2]. (b) R(A, B, C, D), F = {AB → C, C → D, D → A} [3]. (c) R(City, Street, Zip), F = {City Street → Zip, Zip → City} [3]. (d) R(S, C, N, T, G) with F = {S → N, C → T, SC → G} [2].\n\n**Q5. BCNF decomposition.** Decompose R(A, B, C, D, E) with F = {A → B, BC → D, D → E} into BCNF. Show each step, verify losslessness and say which dependencies are lost [10].\n\n**Q6. 3NF synthesis.** For the same R and F: (a) give a minimal cover [3]; (b) synthesise a 3NF decomposition [4]; (c) is it in BCNF? Is it dependency-preserving? [3]\n\n**Q7. Minimal cover and lossless join.** (a) Find a minimal cover of F = {A → BC, B → C, A → B, AB → C} [4]. (b) For R(A, B, C) with A → B and B → C, decide whether the decompositions {AB, BC}, {AB, AC} and {AC, BC} are lossless; give a counterexample instance for the lossy one [6].\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 2\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Entities: Doctor, Patient, Department. Relationships: Appointment (M:N between Patient and Doctor, with attributes date, time, diagnosis) and WorksIn (N:1 from Doctor to Department; total participation of Doctor). The multivalued attribute is Patient.phone.\n(b)\n```\nDepartment(dept_name PK, building)\nDoctor(doc_id PK, name, specialty, dept_name FK -> Department)\nPatient(pat_id PK, name, date_of_birth)\nPatientPhone(pat_id FK -> Patient, phone, PK(pat_id, phone))\nAppointment(pat_id FK, doc_id FK, appt_date, appt_time, diagnosis,\n            PK(pat_id, doc_id, appt_date, appt_time))\n```\n(c) Add a nullable foreign key `head_doc_id` in Department referencing Doctor (a 1:1 relationship), with a constraint that the head belongs to that department. (Circular foreign keys need the column to be nullable or the constraint to be deferred.)\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Closure: repeat \"if the left side is in the set, add the right side\" until nothing changes.\nA+ : start {A}; A → B adds B; nothing else applies (BC needs C, D needs D): **{A, B}**.\nBC+ : start {B, C}; BC → D adds D; D → E adds E: **{B, C, D, E}**.\nAC+ : start {A, C}; A → B adds B; BC → D adds D; D → E adds E: **{A, B, C, D, E}**.\nD+ : start {D}; D → E adds E: **{D, E}**.\n(b) Step 1: A and C never appear on a right-hand side, so they belong to every key.\nStep 2: AC+ = R, so AC is a key; nothing smaller works because A+ = {A, B} and C+ = {C} are not all of R.\nStep 3: **AC is the only candidate key** (any key must contain AC).\n(c) AC → E follows: E is in AC+. BC → E also follows because E is in BC+ = {B, C, D, E}.\n\nStep 1: B appears on no right-hand side, so B is in every key.\nStep 2: closures: B+ = {B} (no FD applies). AB+: AB → C adds C; C → D adds D; D → A (already there): {A, B, C, D}, a key. BC+: C → D adds D; D → A adds A; {A, B, C, D}: a key. BD+: D → A adds A; AB → C adds C: {A, B, C, D}: a key. CD+ = {C, D, A} (no B): not a key. AD+ = {A, D}: not a key.\nStep 3: the **candidate keys are AB, BC and BD** (all minimal, since B alone is not a key), and every attribute (A, B, C, D) is prime.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Key: A (A+ = ABC). B → C: B is not a superkey and C is not prime, so 3NF fails. No partial dependency (the key is a single attribute), so the relation is in **2NF** only.\n(b) Keys AB, BC, BD (Q3), all attributes prime. Every FD satisfies the 3NF condition (the right-hand side is prime), so it is in **3NF**. C → D has a non-superkey left side, so it is **not in BCNF**.\n(c) The keys are (City, Street) and (Street, Zip). Zip → City: Zip is not a superkey but City is prime, so **3NF**, and not BCNF.\n(d) The only key is SC. S → N and C → T are partial dependencies on a part of the key, so the relation violates 2NF: it is in **1NF** only.\n\n**Q5.**\nKey: AC. Violations: A → B (A is not a superkey) and D → E; BC → D has BC as a non-superkey too.\nStep 1: split on A → B. A+ = {A, B}. R1(A, B) and R2 = R - (A+ - A) = (A, C, D, E).\nStep 2: in R2 the FDs that survive projection are AC → D (derived through A → B and BC → D), D → E and so on. D → E violates BCNF (D+ = {D, E} is not a superkey of R2). Split: R21(D, E) and R22(A, C, D).\nStep 3: R22(A, C, D) has the key AC with AC → D, so it is in BCNF; R1(A, B) with A → B and R21(D, E) with D → E are in BCNF.\n**Result: (A, B), (D, E), (A, C, D).** Lossless: each split had the common attributes as a key of one part (A in R1; D in R21). Lost dependency: **BC → D** (F' = {A → B, D → E, AC → D} gives BC+ = {B, C}).\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) The right sides are single attributes already; no left side is reducible (BC → D: neither B+ = B nor C+ = C yields D); no FD is redundant. The minimal cover is **F itself**: {A → B, BC → D, D → E}.\n(b) One relation per FD: R1(A, B), R2(B, C, D), R3(D, E). None contains the key AC, so add R4(A, C). **Result: (A, B), (B, C, D), (D, E), (A, C).**\n(c) Each relation is in BCNF (R2 has the key BC, because BC → D, and in R2 the only FD is BC → D). It is dependency-preserving (every FD sits inside a relation) and lossless (a key relation is included). Here 3NF synthesis gives a better outcome than the BCNF decomposition of Q5.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Split: A → B, A → C, B → C, A → B, AB → C. Remove the duplicate A → B. AB → C: B → C gives C from B alone, so A is extraneous and AB → C reduces to B → C (a duplicate). A → C is redundant because A → B and B → C imply it. The minimal cover is **{A → B, B → C}**.\n(b) {AB, BC}: the common attribute B is a key of BC (B → C), so it is **lossless**. {AB, AC}: the common attribute A is a key of AB (A → B), so it is **lossless**. {AC, BC}: the common attribute C is a key of neither (C+ = {C}), so it is **lossy**. Counterexample: R = {(a1, b1, c1), (a2, b2, c1)} satisfies the FDs. π_AC = {(a1, c1), (a2, c1)} and π_BC = {(b1, c1), (b2, c1)}; their join has 4 tuples, including the spurious (a1, b2, c1) and (a2, b1, c1).\n\n# Assignment 3: Transactions, Recovery, Indexing and Query Processing (Chapters 5 to 8)\n\n**Q1. Serializability.** For each schedule draw the precedence graph, decide conflict-serializability and give an equivalent serial order if one exists: (a) S1: r1(A) w1(A) r2(A) w2(A) r1(B) w1(B) r2(B) w2(B) [2]. (b) S2: r1(A) r2(A) w1(A) w2(A) [2]. (c) S3: w1(A) r2(A) w2(B) r1(B) [2]. (d) S4: r2(B) r1(A) w2(B) r3(B) w1(A) w3(A) r2(A) w3(B) [4].\n\n**Q2. Locking.** T1: read A, then write B. T2: read B, then write A. (a) Write a strict-2PL interleaving that deadlocks, draw the wait-for graph and show how a victim is chosen [5]. (b) Show a schedule that is serializable but cannot be produced by 2PL, or explain why 2PL needs more waiting than necessary [2]. (c) Explain wait-die with timestamps T1 = 5 and T2 = 9 for this conflict [3].\n\n**Q3. Isolation.** (a) Define lost update, dirty read, non-repeatable read and phantom with a short example each [4]. (b) For each isolation level (READ UNCOMMITTED, READ COMMITTED, REPEATABLE READ, SERIALIZABLE) state which of the three read anomalies the SQL standard allows [3]. (c) Explain write skew with the on-call doctors example and why snapshot isolation allows it [3]. (d) State how PostgreSQL 17 differs from the standard table in (b) [2].\n\n**Q4. Recovery.** The log is: (1) <T1 start> (2) <T1, A, 10, 20> (3) <T2 start> (4) <T2, B, 5, 8> (5) <T1 commit> (6) <T3 start> (7) <T3, C, 7, 9> (8) <T2, B, 8, 12> (9) <T3 commit> (10) crash. (a) Which transactions are committed? [2] (b) State the redo and undo actions and the final values of A, B, C if the initial values are A = 10, B = 5, C = 7 [5] (c) Why must the log record be written before the data page, and what does a checkpoint do? [3]\n\n**Q5. B+ trees.** (a) A block is 8192 bytes, a key 12 bytes and a pointer 8 bytes. Find the maximum n, the node capacities and the minimum and maximum height for 50 million keys [5]. (b) For n = 4 (at most 3 keys per node) insert 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 15, 25, 35 in order, drawing the tree after the key sets that cause splits [5].\n\n**Q6. Choosing access paths.** A table of 2,000,000 records of 200 bytes lives in 8192-byte blocks. (a) How many blocks does a full scan read? [2] (b) A query selects the single record with a given id: compare a scan with a B+ tree lookup (height 3) [2] (c) Would you index a column with only two distinct values? Explain [2] (d) Explain why `WHERE UPPER(name) = 'AMA'` cannot use an index on name and how to fix it [2] (e) Compare B+ tree and hash indexes for range queries [2].\n\n**Q7. Join and sort costs.** Relation R has 1200 blocks and S has 300 blocks; the buffer has M = 22 blocks. (a) Cost of block nested-loop join with each relation as the outer [4] (b) Cost of a hash join [2] (c) Cost of externally sorting a 5000-block file with 51 buffer blocks, and the number of passes [4].\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 3\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) All conflicts go from T1 to T2 (the pairs on A and on B): the graph is T1 → T2, acyclic: **serializable, equivalent to T1, T2**.\n(b) r1(A) < w2(A) gives T1 → T2; r2(A) < w1(A) gives T2 → T1: a cycle, **not serializable** (a lost update).\n(c) w1(A) < r2(A) gives T1 → T2; w2(B) < r1(B) gives T2 → T1: a cycle, **not serializable**.\n(d) T1: r1(A) w1(A); T2: r2(B) w2(B) r2(A); T3: r3(B) w3(A) w3(B). Conflicts: w1(A) before r2(A): T1 → T2; r1(A) and w1(A) before w3(A): T1 → T3; w2(B) before r3(B) and w3(B): T2 → T3; w3(A) before r2(A): T3 → T2. The edges T2 → T3 and T3 → T2 form a cycle, so the schedule is **not conflict-serializable**.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Interleaving: T1 S-lock(A), read A; T2 S-lock(B), read B; T1 requests X(B) and waits for T2; T2 requests X(A) and waits for T1. The wait-for graph T1 → T2 → T1 contains a cycle: **deadlock**. The system aborts one transaction as victim (usually the youngest or the one that has done the least work), releasing its locks; the other proceeds and the victim restarts.\n(b) 2PL is conservative: it guarantees serializability but also forbids some serializable schedules (for example those where a transaction releases a lock early and acquires another lock later). So it may block more than strictly necessary.\n(c) With wait-die, an older transaction (smaller timestamp) may wait for a younger one, while a younger transaction requesting a lock held by an older one **dies** (aborts and restarts with its original timestamp). T1 (5) is older than T2 (9). If T1 requests a lock held by T2, T1 waits. If T2 requests a lock held by T1, T2 aborts. A cycle of waits cannot form because waits go only from older to younger.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: **Lost update**: T1 and T2 both read A = 100; T1 writes 110; T2 writes 120; T1's update is lost.\nStep 2: **Dirty read**: T1 writes A = 50 (uncommitted); T2 reads 50; T1 aborts; T2 used a value that never existed.\nStep 3: **Non-repeatable read**: T1 reads A = 100; T2 updates A to 90 and commits; T1 reads again and gets 90.\nStep 4: **Phantom**: T1 counts the rows with dept = 'CE' (5); T2 inserts a CE student and commits; T1 counts again (6).\n(b) READ UNCOMMITTED: dirty reads, non-repeatable reads and phantoms are all possible. READ COMMITTED: non-repeatable reads and phantoms possible. REPEATABLE READ: only phantoms possible. SERIALIZABLE: none.\n(c) Rule: at least one doctor must be on call. Both doctors start in a snapshot where two are on call; each checks \"at least 2 on call\", sets themselves off call and commits. They write different rows, so snapshot isolation sees no write-write conflict, and both commit: zero doctors on call, a state no serial execution could produce. SERIALIZABLE (SSI or locking) prevents it.\n(d) Tested on PostgreSQL 17 with two concurrent sessions. **READ UNCOMMITTED behaves exactly like READ COMMITTED**: dirty reads are impossible at every level. **READ COMMITTED** (the default, confirmed with `SHOW default_transaction_isolation`) allows non-repeatable reads and phantoms (a count went 2 to 3). **REPEATABLE READ** is a snapshot: no non-repeatable reads and, unlike the standard, **no phantoms** either (the count stayed 2); a concurrent writer to the same row gets `could not serialize access due to concurrent update`, so lost updates are prevented by aborting the second transaction, and the application must retry. Write skew still happens at REPEATABLE READ (both transactions committed, 0 doctors on call). **SERIALIZABLE** aborted one of the two (1 doctor remained on call). With a single atomic statement `UPDATE acc SET bal = bal + 10`, two concurrent transactions at READ COMMITTED correctly gave 120, because the second waits for the first lock and re-reads the new value.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) T1 and T3 are committed; T2 has no commit record.\n(b) Step 1: committed: T1 and T3 (redo); uncommitted: T2 (undo).\nStep 2: **Redo** (repeat the updates of committed transactions in log order): record 2: A = 20; record 7: C = 9.\nStep 3: **Undo** the uncommitted T2 in reverse log order: record 8 restores B = 8 (its old value), then record 4 restores B = 5.\nFinal: **A = 20, B = 5, C = 9**.\n(c) Write-ahead logging guarantees that the information to undo or redo an update is on disk before the page it changes can reach disk; without it a crash could leave a changed page with no record of how to undo it. A **checkpoint** forces the log and records the active transactions and dirty pages so that recovery need only scan the log from the last checkpoint rather than from the beginning.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: a node has n pointers and n - 1 keys: 8n + 12 (n - 1) ≤ 8192.\nStep 2: 20n ≤ 8204, so n ≤ 410.2: **n = 410** (8 × 410 + 12 × 409 = 3280 + 4908 = 8188 bytes).\nStep 3: an internal node has up to 410 children and 409 keys; the minimum is ⌈410/2⌉ = **205** children. A leaf holds up to **409** keys and at least ⌈409/2⌉ = **205** keys.\nStep 4 (best case, all nodes full): leaves = 50,000,000/409 = 122,250 (rounded up); nodes above = 122,250/410 = 299 (rounded up); then one root (299 ≤ 410): 3 levels, **height 3**.\nStep 5 (worst case, nodes half full): leaves = 50,000,000/205 = 243,903; the level above = 243,903/205 = 1190; the next = 1190/205 = 6; then the root: 4 levels, **height 4**.\n(b) Leaf capacity 3 keys; a full leaf of 4 splits into two leaves of 2.\nStep 1: 10, 20, 30 fill one leaf [10, 20, 30].\nStep 2: insert 40: overflow [10, 20, 30, 40], split into [10, 20] and [30, 40], root [30].\nStep 3: insert 50: leaf [30, 40, 50]. Insert 60: overflow, split into [30, 40] and [50, 60]; root [30, 50].\nStep 4: insert 15: leaf [10, 15, 20]. Insert 25: overflow [10, 15, 20, 25], split into [10, 15] and [20, 25]; root [20, 30, 50].\nStep 5: insert 35: goes to the leaf [30, 40] → [30, 35, 40].\n**Final tree**: root [20, 30, 50]; leaves [10, 15], [20, 25], [30, 35, 40], [50, 60].\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: records per block = ⌊8192/200⌋ = 40 (40 × 200 = 8000 bytes).\nStep 2: blocks = 2,000,000/40 = **50,000 blocks**.\n(b) A scan reads up to 50,000 blocks (about 25,000 on average if the record is present, found halfway); a B+ tree lookup reads 3 index blocks plus 1 data block = **4 blocks**.\n(c) Generally no: a column with two values has poor selectivity (about half the table matches), so the index would return as many rows as a scan reads, with extra random I/O. (An exception: a skewed column where one value is rare and queries look for it; use a partial index.)\n(d) The index is on name, but the predicate compares UPPER(name), a function of the column, so entries are not ordered by it. Fix: create a **function-based index** on UPPER(name), or store names case-normalised, or use a case-insensitive collation.\n(e) A B+ tree keeps keys sorted and its leaves linked, so it supports range queries (find the first key, then scan leaves). A hash index scatters keys and supports equality only.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Block nested-loop join: cost = b_outer + ⌈b_outer/(M - 2)⌉ × b_inner, with M - 2 = 20 blocks per chunk.\nStep 1: S as the outer: 300 + ⌈300/20⌉ × 1200 = 300 + 15 × 1200 = 300 + 18,000 = **18,300**.\nStep 2: R as the outer: 1200 + ⌈1200/20⌉ × 300 = 1200 + 60 × 300 = 1200 + 18,000 = **19,200**. The smaller outer relation wins.\n(b) Hash join (with enough memory to partition): 3 (b_R + b_S) = 3 (1200 + 300) = **4500** block transfers (read and write the partitions, then read them again): far cheaper.\n(c) Step 1: runs after pass 0: ⌈5000/51⌉ = 99 runs (98.04 rounded up).\nStep 2: each merge pass merges M - 1 = 50 runs: ⌈log_50 99⌉ = **2** merge passes (50 < 99 ≤ 2500).\nStep 3: every pass (run formation plus 2 merge passes = 3 passes) reads and writes the whole file: cost = 2 × 5000 × 3 = **30,000** block transfers.\n","exam":"# Database Systems: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Total: 100 marks. A one-page formula sheet is allowed. SQL questions use standard SQL (SQLite or PostgreSQL syntax is accepted).\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n**Shop database** (used in B1, B2 and C1)\n- customer(cid, name, city): (1, Adwoa, Accra), (2, Bekoe, Kumasi), (3, Cobbina, Accra), (4, Dede, Tamale), (5, Efua, Kumasi).\n- product(pid, pname, price, category): (10, Keyboard, 25.0, Accessory), (11, Mouse, 12.5, Accessory), (12, Monitor, 140.0, Display), (13, Laptop, 620.0, Computer), (14, Cable, 4.0, Accessory).\n- orders(oid, cid, odate): (100, 1, 2025-01-05), (101, 1, 2025-02-10), (102, 2, 2025-02-11), (103, 3, 2025-03-01), (104, 2, 2025-03-15), (105, 5, 2025-03-20).\n- item(oid, pid, qty), primary key (oid, pid): (100, 10, 2), (100, 11, 1), (101, 13, 1), (102, 12, 2), (102, 14, 5), (103, 10, 1), (103, 12, 1), (104, 11, 4), (105, 13, 1), (105, 14, 3).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Physical data independence means we can change\nA) the queries without changing the schema  B) the storage structures and indexes without changing the application programs  C) the schema without changing the data  D) the users without changing the data\n\n**2.** The relational algebra operator that chooses rows satisfying a condition is\nA) projection π  B) selection σ  C) join ⋈  D) rename ρ\n\n**3.** If R has 5 tuples and S has 7 tuples, R × S has\nA) 12 tuples  B) 7 tuples  C) 35 tuples  D) 5 tuples\n\n**4.** A FULL OUTER JOIN keeps\nA) only matching rows  B) unmatched rows of the left table only  C) unmatched rows of the right table only  D) unmatched rows of both tables\n\n**5.** Which SELECT clause is evaluated first?\nA) FROM  B) SELECT  C) ORDER BY  D) HAVING\n\n**6.** The aggregates SUM and AVG\nA) treat NULL as zero  B) fail when a NULL is present  C) ignore NULL values  D) return NULL if any value is NULL\n\n**7.** If the subquery of `x NOT IN (subquery)` returns a NULL, then for a non-matching x the result is\nA) TRUE  B) never TRUE (UNKNOWN)  C) FALSE  D) an error\n\n**8.** A window function such as RANK() OVER (...)\nA) is the same as GROUP BY  B) removes duplicates  C) can be used only in WHERE  D) computes a value per row without collapsing rows\n\n**9.** A weak entity\nA) has no primary key of its own and depends on an owner entity  B) never has attributes  C) is always in a 1:1 relationship  D) has a multivalued key\n\n**10.** A many-to-many relationship is mapped to\nA) a foreign key on one side  B) a multivalued attribute  C) a separate table containing both keys  D) a view\n\n**11.** Which of these is NOT one of Armstrong's axioms?\nA) reflexivity  B) augmentation  C) transitivity  D) idempotence\n\n**12.** Every relation in BCNF is also in\nA) 1NF only  B) 3NF  C) none of the other normal forms  D) 4NF necessarily\n\n**13.** A minimal cover requires that every right-hand side\nA) is a single attribute  B) is a key  C) is empty  D) is the whole relation\n\n**14.** The BCNF decomposition algorithm always gives\nA) dependency preservation  B) 3NF only  C) a lossless join, but it may lose dependencies  D) a smaller number of relations than 3NF synthesis\n\n**15.** T1 reads a value written by T2 which later aborts. This anomaly is\nA) a dirty read  B) a phantom  C) a lost update  D) write skew\n\n**16.** A cascadeless schedule is one in which\nA) transactions never conflict  B) transactions read only data written by committed transactions  C) all transactions are serial  D) no deadlock occurs\n\n**17.** In the wait-die scheme, if an older transaction requests a lock held by a younger one, the older transaction\nA) aborts  B) kills the younger transaction  C) is granted the lock at once  D) waits\n\n**18.** In ARIES, the redo pass\nA) undoes uncommitted work  B) deletes the log  C) repeats history from the log  D) forces all pages\n\n**19.** In a B+ tree of order n = 10, a non-root internal node has at least how many children?\nA) 3  B) 5  C) 7  D) 10\n\n**20.** According to the CAP theorem, during a network partition a distributed system must choose between\nA) consistency and availability  B) speed and cost  C) atomicity and durability  D) reads and writes\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** On the shop database write a relational algebra expression and the result for \"names of customers living in Accra who ordered a Monitor (pid 12)\".\n\n**B2. (5)** Write a query listing every product with the total quantity sold (0 if never sold), most sold first, and give the result.\n\n**B3. (5)** The table t(a) contains the values 1, 2, NULL. Give the results of `SELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(a), SUM(a), AVG(a) FROM t;`, `SELECT a FROM t WHERE a = NULL;` and `SELECT a FROM t WHERE a IS NULL;`, and explain.\n\n**B4. (5)** Map this ER fragment to tables: Supplier (id, name) supplies Part (code, description) with the attribute unit_price on the relationship; a supplier may supply many parts and a part may have many suppliers; a supplier has several phone numbers. Give the schema with keys.\n\n**B5. (5)** R(A, B, C, D) with F = {A → B, B → C, C → D}. Find the candidate keys, the highest normal form and a lossless, dependency-preserving 3NF decomposition.\n\n**B6. (5)** Decide whether the schedule r1(A) r2(B) w1(B) w2(A) is conflict-serializable. Draw the precedence graph and explain.\n\n**B7. (5)** A crash leaves this log: <T1 start>, <T1, X, 10, 20>, <T2 start>, <T2, Y, 30, 40>, <T1 commit>, <T3 start>, <T3, X, 20, 25>. Initial X = 10, Y = 30. State which transactions are redone and undone and the final values of X and Y.\n\n**B8. (5)** Compute the cost in block transfers of externally sorting a file of 3000 blocks with 31 buffer blocks, and give the number of runs and merge passes.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. SQL on the shop database.**\n(a) Define a view order_total(oid, cid, total) giving the value of each order [2]. (b) Using the view, list the names of customers with an order above the average order value, and give the average [3]. (c) For each customer's orders, show a running total of spend in date order using a window function, and give the result [3]. (d) Revenue per product category, largest first [2].\n\n**C2. Normalisation.**\nA university stores Enrolment(StudentID, StudentName, CourseID, CourseTitle, Instructor, Office, Grade) with FDs: StudentID → StudentName; CourseID → CourseTitle, Instructor; Instructor → Office; StudentID CourseID → Grade.\n(a) Find the candidate key and prove it using closure [2]. (b) List the anomalies caused by the current design [2]. (c) Identify which FDs violate 2NF and which violate 3NF [2]. (d) Decompose into 3NF (and BCNF), showing the keys of each relation, and show that the decomposition is lossless [4].\n\n**C3. Transactions.**\n(a) For each schedule give the precedence graph and an equivalent serial order if one exists: S1: r1(A) w1(A) r2(A) w2(A) r1(B) w1(B) r2(B) w2(B); S2: r1(X) r2(X) w2(X) r3(Y) w1(Y) w3(Y); S3: r1(A) r2(A) w2(B) r3(B) w3(C) w1(C) [6]. (b) Three transactions deadlock: T1 holds A and requests B; T2 holds B and requests C; T3 holds C and requests A. Draw the wait-for graph and explain how to resolve it [2]. (c) Why does strict 2PL give cascadeless schedules? [2]\n\n**C4. Indexing.**\n(a) With a block size of 8192 bytes, 4-byte keys and 8-byte pointers find the order n of a B+ tree and the minimum and maximum height for 20 million keys [4]. (b) For n = 4 (at most 3 keys per node) insert 5, 15, 25, 35, 45, 20, 30, 40 in order and draw the final tree [4]. (c) Explain why an index on a column of two distinct values is rarely useful, and when a composite index (a, b) helps a query on b only [2].\n\n**C5. Recovery and distributed databases.**\n(a) The log is: (1) <T1 start> (2) <T1, A, 100, 150> (3) <T2 start> (4) <T2, B, 200, 250> (5) <checkpoint: active T1, T2> (6) <T1 commit> (7) <T3 start> (8) <T3, C, 300, 350> (9) <T2, B, 250, 300> (10) <T2 commit> (11) <T3, A, 150, 175> (12) crash. Initial values A = 100, B = 200, C = 300. State the committed and uncommitted transactions, the redo and undo actions and the final values [5]. (b) Describe the three passes of ARIES [3]. (c) Describe two-phase commit and its blocking problem [2].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **C** · 4 **D** · 5 **A** · 6 **C** · 7 **B** · 8 **D** · 9 **A** · 10 **C** · 11 **D** · 12 **B** · 13 **A** · 14 **C** · 15 **A** · 16 **B** · 17 **D** · 18 **C** · 19 **B** · 20 **A**\n\nBrief reasons: 3. 5 × 7 = 35. 6. They skip NULLs. 7. NOT IN with a NULL gives UNKNOWN for non-members. 12. BCNF is stricter than 3NF. 17. An older transaction waits for a younger one; a younger one requesting from an older one dies. 19. ceil(10/2) = 5.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** π_name(σ_{city='Accra'}(customer) ⋈ orders ⋈ σ_{pid=12}(item)) (the natural joins are on cid and on oid). Customers with a Monitor in an order: Bekoe (order 102) and Cobbina (order 103); of these only Cobbina lives in Accra. **Result: {Cobbina}**.\n\n**B2.**\n```sql\nSELECT p.pname, COALESCE(SUM(i.qty), 0) AS sold\nFROM product p LEFT JOIN item i ON i.pid = p.pid\nGROUP BY p.pid ORDER BY sold DESC, p.pname;\n```\nResult: Cable 8, Mouse 5, Keyboard 3, Monitor 3, Laptop 2.\n\n**B3.** `COUNT(*)` = **3**, `COUNT(a)` = **2**, `SUM(a)` = **3**, `AVG(a)` = **1.5**. `WHERE a = NULL` returns **no rows**: a comparison with NULL is UNKNOWN, and WHERE keeps only TRUE. `WHERE a IS NULL` returns **one row** (NULL). Aggregates other than `COUNT(*)` ignore NULLs.\n\n**B4.**\n```\nSupplier(sid PK, name)\nSupplierPhone(sid FK -> Supplier, phone, PK(sid, phone))\nPart(code PK, description)\nSupplies(sid FK -> Supplier, code FK -> Part, unit_price, PK(sid, code))\n```\nThe M:N relationship becomes the Supplies table with its attribute unit_price; the multivalued phone attribute becomes its own table.\n\n**B5.** A appears on no right-hand side, so it is in every key; A+ = {A, B, C, D}, so **A is the only candidate key**. B → C: B is not a superkey and C is not prime, which violates 3NF; there is no partial dependency (single-attribute key), so the relation is in **2NF**. 3NF synthesis: the minimal cover is F, giving **(A, B), (B, C), (C, D)**; (A, B) contains the key A. This decomposition is lossless (the common attribute is a key of one side at each join: B in (B, C), C in (C, D)) and dependency-preserving; each relation is in BCNF.\n\n**B6.** T1: r1(A), w1(B). T2: r2(B), w2(A). r1(A) before w2(A): conflict, T1 → T2. r2(B) before w1(B): conflict, T2 → T1. The precedence graph has the cycle T1 → T2 → T1, so the schedule is **not conflict-serializable** (neither the order T1, T2 nor T2, T1 gives the same result).\n\n**B7.** Step 1: committed: **T1** (its commit record is in the log); uncommitted: **T2** and **T3**.\nStep 2: redo T1's update: X = 20.\nStep 3: undo in reverse log order: T3's update of X (20 → 25): X back to 20; then T2's update of Y (30 → 40): Y back to 30.\nFinal: **X = 20, Y = 30**. (X is 20 because T1's committed update survives and T3's update to 25 is undone, restoring T1's value.)\n\n**B8.** Step 1: runs after pass 0 (each run fills the 31 buffers): ⌈3000/31⌉ = **97** runs (96.77 rounded up).\nStep 2: each merge pass merges M - 1 = 30 runs: ⌈log_30 97⌉ = **2** passes (30 < 97 ≤ 900).\nStep 3: every pass (run formation plus 2 merge passes = 3) reads and writes the whole file: cost = 2 × 3000 × 3 = **18,000** block transfers.\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a)\n```sql\nCREATE VIEW order_total AS\nSELECT o.oid, o.cid, SUM(i.qty * p.price) AS total\nFROM orders o JOIN item i ON i.oid = o.oid JOIN product p ON p.pid = i.pid\nGROUP BY o.oid;\n```\nContents: (100, 1, 62.5), (101, 1, 620), (102, 2, 300), (103, 3, 165), (104, 2, 50), (105, 5, 632).\n(b)\n```sql\nSELECT name FROM customer\nWHERE cid IN (SELECT cid FROM order_total\n              WHERE total > (SELECT AVG(total) FROM order_total));\n```\nStep 1: the average is (62.5 + 620 + 300 + 165 + 50 + 632)/6 = 1829.5/6 = **304.92**.\nStep 2: orders above it: 101 (620) and 105 (632), belonging to customers 1 (Adwoa) and 5 (Efua). Result: **Adwoa, Efua**.\n(c)\n```sql\nSELECT c.name, o.oid, ot.total,\n       SUM(ot.total) OVER (PARTITION BY c.cid ORDER BY o.odate) AS running\nFROM customer c JOIN orders o ON o.cid = c.cid JOIN order_total ot ON ot.oid = o.oid\nORDER BY c.name, o.odate;\n```\nResult: (Adwoa, 100, 62.5, 62.5), (Adwoa, 101, 620, 682.5), (Bekoe, 102, 300, 300), (Bekoe, 104, 50, 350), (Cobbina, 103, 165, 165), (Efua, 105, 632, 632).\n(d)\n```sql\nSELECT category, SUM(i.qty * p.price) AS rev\nFROM product p JOIN item i ON i.pid = p.pid GROUP BY category ORDER BY rev DESC;\n```\nResult: Computer 1240.0 (2 laptops), Display 420.0 (3 monitors), Accessory 169.5 (Keyboard 3 × 25 = 75, Mouse 5 × 12.5 = 62.5, Cable 8 × 4 = 32).\n\n**C2.**\n(a) StudentID and CourseID appear on no right-hand side, so both are in every key. {StudentID, CourseID}+: StudentID → StudentName; CourseID → CourseTitle, Instructor; Instructor → Office; StudentID CourseID → Grade: all 7 attributes. Neither StudentID+ = {StudentID, StudentName} nor CourseID+ = {CourseID, CourseTitle, Instructor, Office} is complete, so **(StudentID, CourseID) is the only candidate key**.\n(b) Update anomaly: a student's name is repeated in every enrolment (and the course title and instructor office as well). Insertion anomaly: a course cannot be recorded until someone enrols; an instructor's office cannot be stored without a course. Deletion anomaly: deleting the last enrolment in a course loses the course and instructor information.\n(c) **2NF violations (partial dependencies)**: StudentID → StudentName and CourseID → CourseTitle, Instructor (and through it Office). **3NF violation (transitive)**: Instructor → Office (CourseID → Instructor → Office; Instructor is not a superkey and Office is not prime).\n(d) Decomposition:\n```\nStudent(StudentID PK, StudentName)\nCourse(CourseID PK, CourseTitle, Instructor FK)\nInstructor(Instructor PK, Office)\nEnrolment(StudentID FK, CourseID FK, Grade, PK(StudentID, CourseID))\n```\nEvery FD has a superkey (the relation's key) on the left side, so all four relations are in **BCNF** (and hence 3NF). It is dependency-preserving (each FD lies within one relation). It is lossless: joining Enrolment with Student on StudentID (a key of Student), with Course on CourseID (a key of Course) and then with Instructor on Instructor (a key of Instructor) reconstructs the original, since each join attribute is a key of the relation being added.\n\n**C3.**\n(a)\nS1: all conflicts go from T1 to T2 (A: w1 before r2, w2; B: the same). The graph T1 → T2 is acyclic: **serializable, order T1, T2**.\nS2: T1: r1(X) w1(Y); T2: r2(X) w2(X); T3: r3(Y) w3(Y). Edges: r1(X) before w2(X): T1 → T2; r3(Y) before w1(Y): T3 → T1; w1(Y) before w3(Y): T1 → T3. T1 ↔ T3 is a cycle: **not serializable**.\nS3: r1(A) r2(A) w2(B) r3(B) w3(C) w1(C). Conflicts: w2(B) before r3(B): T2 → T3; w3(C) before w1(C): T3 → T1. (The reads of A do not conflict.) The graph T2 → T3 → T1 is acyclic: **serializable, order T2, T3, T1**.\n(b) Wait-for graph: T1 → T2 (T1 waits for B, held by T2), T2 → T3 (waits for C), T3 → T1 (waits for A): a cycle, so a deadlock. Resolution: choose a victim (for example the transaction that has done least work or the youngest), abort it and release its locks (undoing its effects with the log), then restart it. Alternatively prevent deadlocks with wait-die or wound-wait.\n(c) In strict 2PL a transaction holds its exclusive locks until it commits or aborts, so no other transaction can read or overwrite its uncommitted data. Therefore every read sees only committed data (no dirty reads), and an abort cannot cascade to other transactions.\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Step 1: a node has n pointers and n - 1 keys: 8n + 4 (n - 1) ≤ 8192, so 12n ≤ 8196 and n ≤ 683: **n = 683** (8 × 683 + 4 × 682 = 5464 + 2728 = 8192 bytes exactly).\nStep 2: an internal node has up to 683 children and 682 keys, at least ⌈683/2⌉ = **342** children; a leaf holds up to **682** keys and at least ⌈682/2⌉ = **341**.\nStep 3 (best case, nodes full): leaves = ⌈20,000,000/682⌉ = 29,326; above them ⌈29,326/683⌉ = 43; then the root: **height 3**.\nStep 4 (worst case, nodes half full): leaves = ⌈20,000,000/341⌉ = 58,652; above them ⌈58,652/342⌉ = 172; then the root: **height 3**.\nA lookup costs 3 index blocks plus 1 data block.\n(b) Leaf capacity 3 keys. 5, 15, 25 fill one leaf. Insert 35: overflow, split into [5, 15] and [25, 35]; root [25]. Insert 45: leaf [25, 35, 45]. Insert 20: leaf [5, 15, 20]. Insert 30: the leaf [25, 35, 45] becomes [25, 30, 35, 45], an overflow: split into [25, 30] and [35, 45]; the root becomes [25, 35]. Insert 40: leaf [35, 40, 45].\n**Final tree**: root [25, 35]; leaves [5, 15, 20], [25, 30], [35, 40, 45].\n(c) A column with two distinct values has a selectivity of about 50 percent, so an index lookup would still fetch about half of all rows (many random I/Os), which is worse than a sequential scan; a partial index may help if one value is rare. A composite index on (a, b) is sorted by a first, then b, so a query on b alone cannot seek on it; it can still be used by a full index scan if the index covers the query (all needed columns are in the index), or by a skip scan in some systems. Put the most commonly filtered column first.\n\n**C5.**\n(a) The checkpoint shows T1 and T2 active at that time, T3 starts afterwards. Committed: **T1, T2**. Uncommitted: **T3**.\nRedo (repeat the committed updates in log order): record 2: A = 150; record 4: B = 250; record 9: B = 300.\nUndo (uncommitted T3, in reverse order): record 11: A back to 150; record 8: C back to 300.\nFinal values: **A = 150, B = 300, C = 300**. (A = 150 comes from T1's committed update, which T3's undo restores.)\n(b) **Analysis**: scan the log from the last checkpoint to find the transactions that were active at the crash (the \"losers\") and the dirty pages. **Redo**: repeat history from the oldest log record needed, reapplying all logged updates (also those of uncommitted transactions) whose effects are not already on disk, using page LSNs to skip updates already applied. **Undo**: roll back the loser transactions in reverse order, writing compensation log records (CLRs) so that a crash during recovery does not repeat the undo.\n(c) Two-phase commit: the coordinator sends **prepare** to all participants; each votes yes (after forcing its log so it can commit later) or no. If all vote yes, the coordinator logs the decision and sends **commit**; otherwise it sends **abort**. Participants that voted yes cannot unilaterally decide: if the coordinator crashes after the votes, they are **blocked**, holding locks, until it recovers. Three-phase commit or consensus-based protocols (Paxos or Raft logs) reduce blocking.\n","notes":"# Database Systems: Lecture Notes\n\nA database system stores large amounts of data persistently, lets many users query and update it concurrently, and guarantees that it stays correct despite errors and crashes. This course covers the relational model, SQL, database design (normalisation), transactions and concurrency control, recovery, indexing and query processing. All the SQL in these notes was run against SQLite, so the results shown are real. Prerequisites: Discrete Mathematics (sets and relations), Data Structures and Algorithms, and Object-Oriented Programming.\n\n## The sample database used in the notes\nA university registry with three tables (keys declared with PRIMARY KEY):\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE student (sid INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, dept TEXT, year INTEGER);\nCREATE TABLE course  (cid TEXT PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL, credits INTEGER, dept TEXT);\nCREATE TABLE enrol   (sid INTEGER REFERENCES student(sid),\n                      cid TEXT REFERENCES course(cid),\n                      grade INTEGER,\n                      PRIMARY KEY (sid, cid));\n```\n\nstudent: (1, Ama, CE, 2), (2, Kofi, CE, 3), (3, Esi, EE, 2), (4, Yaw, CE, 2), (5, Abena, ME, 1), (6, Kwame, EE, 3), (7, Akua, CE, 1), (8, Nii, CE, 3).\ncourse: CE201 Data Structures (3), CE202 Databases (3), EE210 Circuits (4), MA101 Calculus (4), ME120 Statics (3), CE301 Operating Systems (3).\nenrol (sid, cid, grade): (1, CE201, 78), (1, CE202, 85), (1, MA101, 70), (2, CE201, 64), (2, CE301, 72), (3, EE210, 90), (3, MA101, 66), (4, CE201, 55), (4, CE202, 61), (4, MA101, 49), (5, ME120, 58), (5, MA101, 74), (6, EE210, 81), (7, MA101, 88), (8, CE202, 69), (8, CE301, 77).\n\n# Chapter 1: The Relational Model\n\n## 1.1 Why databases?\nFiles plus application code lead to redundancy, inconsistency, no concurrent access control, no crash recovery and hard-coded access paths. A **database management system (DBMS)** supplies:\n- **Data independence**: logical independence (changing the schema without changing programs) and physical independence (changing storage and indexes without changing queries).\n- A declarative query language (SQL): say **what** you want, and the optimiser decides **how**.\n- **Transactions** with ACID guarantees, concurrency control, recovery, security and integrity constraints.\n\n## 1.2 Definitions\n- A **relation** is a set of tuples over a **schema** R(A1, ..., An) with domains for each attribute; a **table** is its SQL form (a bag, not a set, unless keys or DISTINCT are used).\n- **Degree** = number of attributes; **cardinality** = number of tuples.\n- **Superkey**: a set of attributes that uniquely identifies a tuple. **Candidate key**: a minimal superkey. **Primary key**: the chosen candidate key (not NULL). **Foreign key**: attributes referencing a key of another relation; every non-null value must exist there (**referential integrity**).\n- In enrol, (sid, cid) is the primary key, sid is a foreign key to student and cid is a foreign key to course.\n\n## 1.3 Relational algebra\nProcedural operators on relations (the theoretical foundation of SQL):\n- **Selection** σ_condition(R): choose rows. Example: σ_{dept='CE' ∧ year=2}(student) = {Ama, Yaw}.\n- **Projection** π_attrs(R): choose columns (duplicates removed in the pure algebra).\n- **Union, intersection, difference** (union-compatible relations).\n- **Cartesian product** R × S: with 8 students and 6 courses it has 8 × 6 = 48 tuples.\n- **Natural join** R ⋈ S = π(σ_{R.A=S.A}(R × S)) on common attributes; **theta join** R ⋈_θ S = σ_θ(R × S).\n- **Rename** ρ_{new}(R).\n- **Division** R ÷ S: tuples of R's remaining attributes that are paired with every tuple of S. \"Students enrolled in all CE courses in {CE201, CE202}\" is π_{sid,cid}(enrol) ÷ {CE201, CE202} = {1 (Ama), 4 (Yaw)}.\n- Aggregation γ_{group; functions}(R).\n\nExample query. \"Names of students enrolled in CE202\": π_name(student ⋈ σ_{cid='CE202'}(enrol)) = {Ama, Yaw, Nii}.\n\n**Equivalences** the optimiser uses: σ_{c1 ∧ c2}(R) = σ_{c1}(σ_{c2}(R)); selection commutes with projection when the attributes agree; push selections below joins; join is associative and commutative.\n\n# Chapter 2: Entity-Relationship Modelling\n\n## 2.1 Concepts\n- **Entity**: a thing with an independent existence (Student). **Entity set**: all entities of a type. **Attributes**: simple, composite (address), multivalued (phone numbers), derived (age from date of birth).\n- **Relationship**: an association among entities (Student enrols in Course). Its **cardinality** is 1:1, 1:N or M:N, and its **participation** is total (every entity takes part) or partial.\n- **Weak entity**: no key of its own; identified through an owner entity (a Room within a Building).\n- **Specialisation/generalisation** (ISA): Undergraduate and Postgraduate are kinds of Student.\n\n## 2.2 Mapping ER to relational tables\n1. Strong entity: a table with its simple attributes and primary key; composite attributes are flattened.\n2. Weak entity: a table including the owner's key, with the key = owner key + partial key.\n3. 1:N relationship: put the key of the \"one\" side as a foreign key on the \"N\" side.\n4. M:N relationship: a new table with both keys (and the relationship's attributes). Enrol is the M:N relationship between Student and Course, with the attribute grade.\n5. 1:1 relationship: a foreign key on either side (preferably the side with total participation).\n6. Multivalued attribute: a separate table (entity key, value).\n7. ISA: either one table per class (subclass tables carry the key), or a single table with a type column.\n\nDesign advice: model first with an ER diagram, map to tables, then check the tables with the normal forms of Chapter 4.\n\n# Chapter 3: SQL\n\n## 3.1 Data definition and constraints\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE enrol (\n  sid   INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  cid   TEXT    NOT NULL,\n  grade INTEGER CHECK (grade BETWEEN 0 AND 100),\n  PRIMARY KEY (sid, cid),\n  FOREIGN KEY (sid) REFERENCES student(sid) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (cid) REFERENCES course(cid)\n);\nALTER TABLE student ADD COLUMN email TEXT;\nCREATE INDEX idx_enrol_cid ON enrol(cid);\nDROP TABLE tmp;\n```\nConstraints: NOT NULL, UNIQUE, PRIMARY KEY, CHECK, FOREIGN KEY (with ON DELETE CASCADE, SET NULL or RESTRICT). Data types vary by system (INTEGER, NUMERIC, VARCHAR(n), DATE, TIMESTAMP, BOOLEAN).\n\n## 3.2 Data manipulation\n```sql\nINSERT INTO student VALUES (9, 'Yaa', 'CE', 1);\nINSERT INTO student (sid, name) VALUES (10, 'Kojo');\nUPDATE enrol SET grade = grade + 5 WHERE cid = 'CE202' AND grade < 60;\nDELETE FROM enrol WHERE sid = 4;\n```\nAlways include a WHERE in UPDATE and DELETE unless you intend to change every row.\n\n## 3.3 The SELECT statement\nThe clauses are written SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, ORDER BY, LIMIT but **evaluated** in the order FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY, LIMIT. This explains why a SELECT alias is not visible in WHERE.\n\nSimple selection:\n```sql\nSELECT name FROM student WHERE dept = 'CE' AND year = 2;\n```\nResult: Ama, Yaw.\n\nGrouping and counting:\n```sql\nSELECT dept, COUNT(*) AS n FROM student GROUP BY dept ORDER BY dept;\n```\nResult: (CE, 5), (EE, 2), (ME, 1).\n\n## 3.4 Joins\n```sql\nSELECT s.name, c.title, e.grade\nFROM student s JOIN enrol e ON s.sid = e.sid\n               JOIN course c ON c.cid = e.cid\nWHERE e.grade >= 80\nORDER BY e.grade DESC;\n```\nResult: (Esi, Circuits, 90), (Akua, Calculus, 88), (Ama, Databases, 85), (Kwame, Circuits, 81).\n\nKinds of join:\n- **INNER JOIN** keeps only matching pairs.\n- **LEFT OUTER JOIN** keeps every row of the left table, filling the right columns with NULL when nothing matches.\n- **RIGHT / FULL OUTER JOIN** analogously.\n- **CROSS JOIN** gives the Cartesian product (48 rows for student × course).\n- **Self join** joins a table to itself using aliases: pairs of students in CE201:\n```sql\nSELECT e1.sid, e2.sid FROM enrol e1 JOIN enrol e2\n  ON e1.cid = e2.cid AND e1.sid < e2.sid WHERE e1.cid = 'CE201';\n```\nResult: (1, 2), (1, 4), (2, 4).\n\n## 3.5 Aggregates, GROUP BY and HAVING\nAggregate functions: COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX. **WHERE** filters rows before grouping; **HAVING** filters groups after.\n\n```sql\nSELECT s.name, AVG(e.grade) AS avg_g\nFROM student s JOIN enrol e USING (sid)\nGROUP BY s.sid HAVING AVG(e.grade) > 70 ORDER BY avg_g DESC;\n```\nResult: Akua 88.0, Kwame 81.0, Esi 78.0, Ama 77.67, Nii 73.0.\n\nCourses with their enrolment count, average and top grade, including courses nobody takes (use a LEFT JOIN):\n```sql\nSELECT c.cid, COUNT(e.sid) AS n, ROUND(AVG(e.grade),1) AS avg_g, MAX(e.grade) AS top\nFROM course c LEFT JOIN enrol e ON e.cid = c.cid GROUP BY c.cid;\n```\nResult: CE201 (3, 65.7, 78), CE202 (3, 71.7, 85), CE301 (2, 74.5, 77), EE210 (2, 85.5, 90), MA101 (5, 69.4, 88), ME120 (1, 58.0, 58).\n\nEvery selected column must be either grouped or aggregated.\n\n## 3.6 Subqueries\n```sql\n-- scalar subquery: grades above the Calculus average (69.4)\nSELECT s.name, e.grade FROM student s JOIN enrol e ON s.sid = e.sid\nWHERE e.cid = 'MA101' AND e.grade > (SELECT AVG(grade) FROM enrol WHERE cid = 'MA101');\n```\nResult: (Ama, 70), (Abena, 74), (Akua, 88).\n\n```sql\n-- IN: students who took both CE201 and CE202\nSELECT name FROM student\nWHERE sid IN (SELECT sid FROM enrol WHERE cid = 'CE201')\n  AND sid IN (SELECT sid FROM enrol WHERE cid = 'CE202');\n```\nResult: Ama, Yaw.\n\n```sql\n-- correlated NOT EXISTS: students who did not take CE202\nSELECT name FROM student s\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM enrol e WHERE e.sid = s.sid AND e.cid = 'CE202');\n```\nResult: Kofi, Esi, Abena, Kwame, Akua.\n\n**Division in SQL** with a double NOT EXISTS (\"students who took every CE course among CE201, CE202\"):\n```sql\nSELECT name FROM student s\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (\n  SELECT cid FROM course WHERE cid IN ('CE201','CE202')\n  EXCEPT\n  SELECT cid FROM enrol e WHERE e.sid = s.sid);\n```\nResult: Ama, Yaw.\n\nSet operators: UNION, INTERSECT, EXCEPT (duplicates removed unless UNION ALL). Example: departments of students that also appear as course departments: (CE, EE, ME).\n\n## 3.7 NULL and three-valued logic\nNULL means \"unknown\". Any comparison with NULL is UNKNOWN, and WHERE keeps only TRUE rows.\n- `a = NULL` is never true: use `a IS NULL`.\n- `COUNT(*)` counts rows; `COUNT(a)` counts non-null values; SUM and AVG ignore NULLs. For a column with values 1, 2, NULL: `COUNT(*)` = 3, `COUNT(a)` = 2, SUM = 3, AVG = 1.5.\n- **The NOT IN trap**: if the subquery returns any NULL, `x NOT IN (...)` is never TRUE. With t = {1, 2, NULL} and u = {2, NULL}: `SELECT a FROM t WHERE a NOT IN (SELECT a FROM u)` returns **no rows**, whereas `NOT EXISTS` returns {1, NULL}, and filtering out the NULLs in the subquery returns {1}. Prefer NOT EXISTS.\n- Outer joins create NULLs: always think about what a missing match does to your WHERE and aggregates.\n\n## 3.8 Window functions and CASE\n```sql\nSELECT cid, name, grade, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY cid ORDER BY grade DESC) AS r\nFROM student JOIN enrol USING (sid) WHERE cid IN ('CE201','CE202');\n```\nResult: CE201: Ama 78 (1), Kofi 64 (2), Yaw 55 (3); CE202: Ama 85 (1), Nii 69 (2), Yaw 61 (3). Window functions compute per-row values over a related set of rows without collapsing them.\n\n```sql\nSELECT s.name, CASE WHEN e.grade >= 80 THEN 'A' WHEN e.grade >= 70 THEN 'B'\n                    WHEN e.grade >= 60 THEN 'C' ELSE 'F' END AS letter\nFROM student s JOIN enrol e USING (sid) WHERE cid = 'CE201';\n```\nResult: Ama B, Kofi C, Yaw F.\n\n## 3.9 Views, indexes and programmatic SQL\nA **view** is a stored query: `CREATE VIEW ce_students AS SELECT * FROM student WHERE dept = 'CE';`. Views provide security and logical independence. A **materialised view** stores the result.\n\nUsing a database from Python with parameterised queries (never concatenate user input into SQL; that is **SQL injection**):\n```python\nimport sqlite3\ncon = sqlite3.connect(\"registry.db\")\ncur = con.execute(\"SELECT name FROM student WHERE dept = ? AND year = ?\", (\"CE\", 2))\nprint(cur.fetchall())\ncon.commit()\n```\nAn attacker who controls `name` in `\"... WHERE name = '\" + name + \"'\"` can enter `x' OR '1'='1` and read every row; placeholders send the value separately from the statement, so it can never be parsed as SQL.\n\n# Chapter 4: Database Design Theory\n\n## 4.1 Why normalise?\nA poor design stores facts redundantly and causes anomalies. Consider one table `enrol_flat(sid, name, cid, title, grade)`:\n- **Update anomaly**: changing Ama's name needs every one of her rows updated.\n- **Insertion anomaly**: a new course cannot be stored until a student enrols.\n- **Deletion anomaly**: deleting the last enrolment loses the course title.\n\n## 4.2 Functional dependencies and closure\nA **functional dependency** X → Y holds if any two tuples that agree on X also agree on Y. It is a statement about the meaning of the data (all possible instances), not about the current rows.\n\n**Armstrong's axioms**: reflexivity (Y ⊆ X implies X → Y), augmentation (X → Y implies XZ → YZ), transitivity (X → Y and Y → Z imply X → Z). Derived rules: union, decomposition, pseudo-transitivity.\n\n**Attribute closure** X+: the set of attributes determined by X. Algorithm: start with X+ = X and repeat \"if some L → R has L ⊆ X+ then add R\" until nothing changes. X is a superkey if X+ is all of R.\n\nExample. R(A, B, C, D, E) with F = {A → B, BC → D, D → E}.\n- A+ = {A, B}; BC+ = {B, C, D, E}; D+ = {D, E}.\n- AC+: A → B gives B; then BC → D gives D; D → E gives E; so AC+ = {A, B, C, D, E} and **AC is a key**. Since A and C appear on no right-hand side they are in every key, so AC is the **only** candidate key.\n\nExample. R(A, B, C, D) with F = {AB → C, C → D, D → A}. B appears on no right side so it is in every key. Test AB: AB → C → D → A, so AB+ = ABCD: a key. BC: C → D → A so BC+ = ABCD: a key. BD: D → A, then AB → C: BD+ = ABCD: a key. **Candidate keys: AB, BC, BD.**\n\n## 4.3 Normal forms\n- **1NF**: every attribute is atomic (no repeating groups or arrays in a column).\n- **2NF**: 1NF and no non-prime attribute depends on **part** of a candidate key. (Only relevant when a key is composite.)\n- **3NF**: for every FD X → A, either X is a superkey, or A is a prime attribute (part of some candidate key). Equivalent: no non-prime attribute depends transitively on a key.\n- **BCNF**: for every non-trivial FD X → A, X is a superkey. Stricter than 3NF.\n\nHierarchy: BCNF is contained in 3NF, which is contained in 2NF, which is contained in 1NF.\n\nExample. enrol_flat(S = sid, N = name, C = cid, T = title, G = grade) with F = {S → N, C → T, SC → G}. The only key is SC. S → N violates 2NF (N depends on part of the key), as does C → T. Decompose into student(S, N), course(C, T), enrol(S, C, G): each is in BCNF.\n\nExample of 3NF but not BCNF. R(A, B, C, D) with F = {AB → C, C → D, D → A} and keys AB, BC, BD. The FD C → D has C not a superkey (C+ = ACD, missing B), so R is **not in BCNF**; but D is prime (in key BD), so it is in **3NF** (the other FDs have a superkey on the left or a prime on the right).\n\nAnother classic: R(City, Street, Zip) with CS → Z and Z → C. The keys are CS and SZ. Z → C: Z is not a superkey but C is prime, so it is in 3NF but not BCNF.\n\n## 4.4 Decomposition properties\nA decomposition of R into R1, R2 is:\n- **Lossless-join** if R1 ⋈ R2 = R for every instance. Test for two relations: the common attributes R1 ∩ R2 form a key of R1 or of R2 (equivalently (R1 ∩ R2) → R1 or → R2 follows from F).\n- **Dependency-preserving** if the union of the FDs that can be checked inside each Ri implies all of F.\n\nBCNF decomposition (always lossless, but it may not preserve dependencies): while some Ri has a violating FD X → Y, replace Ri by (X+ ∩ Ri) and (Ri minus (X+ minus X)).\n**3NF synthesis** (always lossless and dependency-preserving): compute a **minimal cover**, make one relation per FD (grouping those with the same left side), and add a relation containing a candidate key if none of the relations contains one.\n\nExample. R(A, B, C, D), F = {AB → C, C → D, D → A}. 3NF synthesis: the minimal cover is F itself, giving R1(A, B, C), R2(C, D), R3(D, A). R1 contains the key AB. The result preserves all three FDs and is lossless. BCNF: split on C → D: R1(A, C, D) (C+ = ACD) and R2(B, C); within R1 the FD D → A violates BCNF, so split into (A, D) and (C, D). The final BCNF schema (A, D), (C, D), (B, C) is lossless, but **AB → C is lost** (it cannot be checked within any single relation).\n\n**Minimal cover**: (1) one attribute on each right side; (2) no extraneous left-side attributes; (3) no redundant FD. Example: F = {A → BC, B → C, A → B, AB → C}. Split: A → B, A → C, B → C, A → B, AB → C. Remove the duplicate A → B. AB → C: B alone determines C, so it reduces to B → C (a duplicate). A → C is redundant: A → B and B → C give it. The minimal cover is **{A → B, B → C}**.\n\n## 4.5 Denormalisation\nNormalisation optimises for updates and consistency; analytical (read-heavy) systems often denormalise (star schemas, precomputed aggregates) to avoid costly joins. Do it deliberately, with the redundancy managed.\n\n# Chapter 5: Transactions and Concurrency Control\n\n## 5.1 ACID\nA **transaction** is a unit of work, a sequence of reads and writes, that is either all done or not at all.\n- **Atomicity**: all or nothing (on a failure the effects are rolled back).\n- **Consistency**: it takes the database from one valid state to another (integrity constraints hold).\n- **Isolation**: concurrent transactions behave as if executed one at a time.\n- **Durability**: once committed, results survive crashes.\n\nTransfer example: debit 30 from account 1 (balance 100) and credit 30 to account 2 (balance 50). Inside the transaction the balances are (70, 80); after a ROLLBACK they are again (100, 50), so no money is lost or created.\n```sql\nBEGIN;\nUPDATE acct SET bal = bal - 30 WHERE id = 1;\nUPDATE acct SET bal = bal + 30 WHERE id = 2;\nCOMMIT;   -- or ROLLBACK;\n```\n\n## 5.2 Anomalies of concurrent execution\n- **Lost update**: T1 and T2 both read A = 100, T1 writes 110, T2 writes 120: T1's update is lost.\n- **Dirty read**: reading data written by a transaction that later aborts.\n- **Non-repeatable read**: a row read twice in a transaction has different values because another transaction committed in between.\n- **Phantom**: the same query returns a different set of rows the second time.\n- **Write skew** under snapshot isolation: two transactions read overlapping data and write disjoint items, violating an invariant (two doctors both go off call).\n\n## 5.3 Schedules and serializability\nA **schedule** interleaves the operations of transactions, keeping each transaction's order. A **serial** schedule runs one transaction at a time. A schedule is **serializable** if it is equivalent to some serial schedule.\n\nTwo operations **conflict** if they belong to different transactions, access the same item and at least one is a write (read-write, write-read, write-write). **Conflict serializability** test: build the **precedence graph** with an edge Ti → Tj if an operation of Ti conflicts with a later operation of Tj. The schedule is conflict-serializable if and only if the graph is **acyclic**; a topological order gives an equivalent serial order.\n\nExample S1: r1(A) w1(A) r2(A) w2(A) r1(B) w1(B) r2(B) w2(B). All conflicts go from T1 to T2, so the graph is just T1 → T2 and S1 is equivalent to the serial order T1, T2.\n\nExample S2 (lost update): r1(A) r2(A) w1(A) w2(A). r1(A) before w2(A) gives T1 → T2; r2(A) before w1(A) gives T2 → T1: a **cycle**, so S2 is not conflict-serializable.\n\nExample S3: w1(A) r2(A) w2(B) r1(B). w1(A) before r2(A): T1 → T2; w2(B) before r1(B): T2 → T1: a cycle, so not serializable.\n\nA schedule is **recoverable** if a transaction commits only after every transaction whose writes it read has committed; it is **cascadeless** if transactions read only committed data.\n\n## 5.4 Two-phase locking (2PL)\nLocks: **shared (S)** for reading, **exclusive (X)** for writing. Compatibility: S with S is allowed; any pair involving X conflicts. **2PL**: each transaction has a **growing** phase (acquires locks) and then a **shrinking** phase (releases locks, never acquires again). 2PL guarantees conflict-serializability. **Strict 2PL** holds all X locks until commit, which also gives cascadeless schedules and is what most systems use.\n\n**Deadlock**: T1 holds a lock on A and waits for B while T2 holds B and waits for A. Handling:\n- **Detection**: build a **wait-for graph** (edge Ti → Tj if Ti waits for Tj); a cycle means a deadlock; abort a victim.\n- **Prevention** with timestamps: **wait-die** (an older transaction waits for a younger; a younger requesting from an older aborts) or **wound-wait**.\n- **Timeouts**.\n\n**Lock granularity**: row, page, table, with intention locks (IS, IX) to coordinate them. A **predicate** or range lock is needed to prevent phantoms.\n\n## 5.5 Isolation levels (SQL standard)\n- **READ UNCOMMITTED**: dirty reads possible.\n- **READ COMMITTED**: no dirty reads; non-repeatable reads and phantoms possible (the default in PostgreSQL and Oracle).\n- **REPEATABLE READ**: no dirty or non-repeatable reads; phantoms possible by the standard (MySQL InnoDB default).\n- **SERIALIZABLE**: no anomalies.\n\n**MVCC (multi-version concurrency control)**: writers create new versions, readers read a consistent snapshot without blocking writers. **Snapshot isolation** prevents lost updates and most anomalies but permits write skew. PostgreSQL's SERIALIZABLE uses serializable snapshot isolation (SSI) to detect it.\n\n**What PostgreSQL really does** (tested on PostgreSQL 17 with concurrent sessions): READ UNCOMMITTED is treated as READ COMMITTED (no dirty reads at any level); the default is READ COMMITTED; REPEATABLE READ is snapshot isolation, so it also prevents phantoms and aborts a transaction that updates a row changed since its snapshot (error \"could not serialize access due to concurrent update\", which the application must retry); only SERIALIZABLE prevents write skew (it aborts one of the two transactions). Deadlocks are detected automatically: one transaction receives \"deadlock detected\" and is rolled back while the other proceeds.\n\n**Optimistic concurrency control**: execute without locks, validate at commit time; good when conflicts are rare.\n\n# Chapter 6: Recovery\n\n## 6.1 Failure types\nTransaction failure (abort), system crash (memory lost, disk intact), media failure (disk lost; recover from backups plus logs).\n\n## 6.2 Write-ahead logging (WAL)\nKeep a sequential **log** of records <T, item, old value, new value>, <T commit>, <T abort> on stable storage. **Rule: the log record must reach disk before the data page it describes (WAL), and all log records of a transaction before its commit record.** Data pages can be written lazily (**no-force**) and uncommitted changes may reach disk (**steal**); WAL makes both safe. After a crash:\n- **Redo** the updates of committed transactions (to ensure durability).\n- **Undo** the updates of uncommitted transactions (to ensure atomicity).\n\n**Checkpoints** limit how far back the log must be scanned. **ARIES** performs three passes: analysis (find dirty pages and active transactions), redo (repeat history from the oldest needed log record) and undo (roll back losers, writing compensation log records).\n\nExample. Log: <T1 start>, <T1, A, 10, 20>, <T2 start>, <T2, B, 5, 8>, <T1 commit>, <crash>. After the crash: T1 is committed, so redo A = 20; T2 is not committed, so undo B back to 5.\n\n# Chapter 7: Storage and Indexing\n\n## 7.1 Storage\nData is stored in fixed-size **blocks (pages)**, typically 4 KB to 16 KB, and the cost of a query is dominated by the number of block transfers (I/O). A table is a **heap file** (unordered) or sorted or clustered on a key. With 100-byte records and 4096-byte blocks, 40 records fit in a block, so a table of 10,000,000 records needs 10^7/40 = **250,000 blocks**; a full scan reads all of them.\n\n## 7.2 Indexes\nAn **index** on a search key maps key values to record locations.\n- **Primary (clustering) index**: the file is sorted on the key. **Secondary index**: on a non-ordering attribute (dense: one entry per record).\n- **Dense** index: an entry for every key value; **sparse**: an entry per block.\n\n## 7.3 B+ trees\nThe dominant index structure. Balanced tree where all leaves are at the same depth, leaves are linked for range scans and each node (except the root) is at least half full. For a node with up to n pointers:\n- Internal nodes have between ceil(n/2) and n children; leaves hold between ceil((n-1)/2) and n - 1 keys.\n- Height is about log_{n/2}(N) at worst.\n\nExample. Block 4096 bytes, key 8 bytes, pointer 8 bytes: the largest n with 8n + 8(n - 1) <= 4096 is **n = 256** (it uses 4088 bytes). A node holds up to 255 keys, and at least 128 pointers (internal) or 128 keys (leaf).\nFor N = 10,000,000 records:\n- Best case (full nodes): 10^7/255 = 39,216 leaves; above: 154 nodes; above: the root. **Height 3**.\n- Worst case (half-full): at most 78,125 leaves; at most 611 nodes; at most 5; then the root: **height 4**.\nA point lookup costs height + 1 block reads (the data block), i.e. 4 or 5, against 250,000 for a scan.\n\n**Measured on PostgreSQL 17** (8192-byte pages, a table of 1,000,000 rows): the heap occupies 9,408 pages and an equality search without an index scans all of them (about 3 parallel workers touched 9,408 buffers); after `CREATE INDEX` the same query read **4 buffers** (3 index pages plus 1 heap page), because the index metadata (`bt_metap` from the pageinspect extension) reports a root level of 2, i.e. a tree of 3 levels. This matches the height + 1 formula. The planner ignored an index on a column with two distinct values (flag): it used a bitmap scan touching 9,346 of the 9,408 heap pages (almost the whole table), and it used a sequential scan for `WHERE upper(name) = 'ABC'` until an index on `upper(name)` was created. A range query reads the leaf level sequentially.\n\nOperations: **search** follows pointers down; **insert** places the key in the leaf and, if the leaf overflows, **splits** it and pushes the middle key up (splits may cascade to the root, increasing the height); **delete** removes the key and, if the node underflows, **borrows from a sibling or merges**.\n\n## 7.4 Hashing\nStatic hashing: bucket = h(key) mod B; equality search in about 1 I/O; no range queries. Example h(k) = k mod 7 for keys 15, 22, 8, 37, 29, 14 gives buckets 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 0: bucket 1 is crowded (overflow chain). **Extendible and linear hashing** grow the directory dynamically. Use B+ trees by default; hash indexes for pure equality lookups.\n\n## 7.5 Choosing indexes\nIndex the columns used in WHERE, JOIN and ORDER BY with selective predicates, and foreign keys. Every index slows writes and uses space. Composite index (a, b) helps filters on a or on a and b but not on b alone. Check the plan with EXPLAIN.\n\n# Chapter 8: Query Processing and Optimisation\n\n## 8.1 Pipeline\nSQL text is parsed, converted to a relational-algebra tree, rewritten (heuristics: push selections and projections down, avoid Cartesian products), optimised using a cost model with statistics (table sizes, distinct values, histograms) to choose the join order and algorithms, then executed.\n\n## 8.2 Join algorithms\nLet R and S occupy b_R and b_S blocks and let M be the number of buffer blocks.\n- **Nested-loop join** (tuple at a time): the worst; b_R + n_R × b_S transfers.\n- **Block nested-loop join**: read R in chunks of M - 2 blocks and scan S for each chunk: b_R + ceil(b_R/(M - 2)) × b_S. Put the **smaller** relation as the outer one.\n- **Index nested-loop join**: for each tuple of R use an index on S's join column.\n- **Sort-merge join**: sort both on the join key, merge: cost of sorting plus b_R + b_S.\n- **Hash join**: partition both by a hash of the key, then join the matching partitions: about 3 (b_R + b_S) transfers.\n\nExample: b_R = 500, b_S = 100, M = 12 (so 10 blocks per chunk). With S as the outer: 100 + ceil(100/10) × 500 = **5100**. With R as the outer: 500 + ceil(500/10) × 100 = **5500**. The smaller outer wins.\n\n## 8.3 External sorting\nWith M buffer blocks and a file of b blocks: pass 0 creates ceil(b/M) sorted runs; each later pass merges M - 1 runs. Total transfers = 2b (ceil(log_{M-1}(b/M)) + 1). Example: b = 1000, M = 11: 91 runs, merge passes ceil(log_10 91) = 2, so 2 × 1000 × 3 = **6000** block transfers.\n\n## 8.4 Reading a plan\n`EXPLAIN` shows the plan: table scans, index lookups, join algorithms, estimated rows and cost. Common tuning moves: add a missing index, rewrite correlated subqueries as joins, avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE (the index cannot be used), select only needed columns, and update statistics.\n\n# Chapter 9: Beyond the Relational Model\n\n## 9.1 NoSQL families\n- **Key-value** stores (Redis, DynamoDB): simple, fast, horizontally scalable.\n- **Document** stores (MongoDB): JSON documents, flexible schema.\n- **Column-family** (Cassandra, HBase): wide rows, high write throughput.\n- **Graph** databases (Neo4j): relationships first-class; traversal queries.\n\n## 9.2 Distributed concerns\n**CAP theorem**: in the presence of a network partition a distributed system must choose between **consistency** and **availability**. Many NoSQL systems provide **eventual consistency** (BASE: basically available, soft state, eventually consistent). **Replication** (leader-follower) improves reads and availability; **sharding** (partitioning) improves capacity. Distributed transactions use **two-phase commit** (a prepare phase where each participant votes, then a commit phase), which can block if the coordinator fails.\n\n## 9.3 Choosing\nUse a relational database when data is structured, relationships matter and correctness (ACID) is critical (most business systems). Consider other models when scale, access patterns or flexibility require it. Many systems use several (polyglot persistence).\n\n## Quick reference\n- Closure algorithm, key: X is a key if X+ = R and no proper subset has this property.\n- 3NF: X → A with X a superkey or A prime. BCNF: X always a superkey.\n- Conflict serializable if and only if the precedence graph is acyclic. Strict 2PL gives serializability and cascadeless schedules.\n- B+ tree fan-out: n = floor((block + key)/(key + pointer)); lookup = height + 1 reads.\n- Block nested-loop cost: b_outer + ceil(b_outer/(M - 2)) × b_inner.\n- Sort cost: 2b (ceil(log_{M-1}(b/M)) + 1).\n","plan":"# Database Systems: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 8 to 10 hours per week (3 reading, 5 to 6 hands-on SQL and design work, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Install SQLite (it ships with Python) and PostgreSQL, and recreate the notes' university registry so that you can run every query yourself. Databases are learned by querying and designing real data sets.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Abraham Silberschatz, Henry Korth and S. Sudarshan, *Database System Concepts*. The standard university textbook (relational model, SQL, design, transactions, storage and indexing).\n- Ramez Elmasri and Shamkant Navathe, *Fundamentals of Database Systems*.\n- Raghu Ramakrishnan and Johannes Gehrke, *Database Management Systems* (\"the Cow book\"). Excellent on indexing, query processing and recovery.\n- Hector Garcia-Molina, Jeffrey Ullman and Jennifer Widom, *Database Systems: The Complete Book*. Detailed design theory.\n- Martin Kleppmann, *Designing Data-Intensive Applications*. The practical modern view (replication, partitioning, consistency).\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- CMU 15-445 Database Systems (Andy Pavlo; lectures and projects are free online); Stanford CS145 and the Stanford Online \"Databases\" course by Jennifer Widom.\n- SQLite, PostgreSQL, DB Fiddle, SQLBolt and Mode's SQL tutorial for practice; the PostgreSQL documentation for EXPLAIN.\n- Relational algebra and normalisation tools: RelaX (relational algebra calculator), and the many online closure and normal form checkers (use them to check your hand work, not to replace it).\n- Datasets: Chinook, Northwind, the Sakila sample database, or the open data sets of your choice.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: The relational model and relational algebra (Ch 1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1. Install SQLite and PostgreSQL and create the registry database.\n\n**Week 2: SQL basics: SELECT, WHERE, joins (Ch 3.1 to 3.4).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2, Q3 (a). Write the queries yourself before checking.\n\n**Week 3: Aggregates, subqueries, NULL (Ch 3.5 to 3.7).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3, Q4. Reproduce the NOT IN trap.\n\n**Week 4: Window functions, views, DDL and programmatic SQL (Ch 3.8, 3.9).** Do: Assignment 1 Q5, Q6, Q7. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week (Chapters 1 to 3 and the first look at design theory).\n\n**Week 5: ER modelling and mapping to tables (Ch 2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1. Design a schema for a small application of your own (a clinic, a library, a shop).\n\n**Week 6: Functional dependencies and closure (Ch 4.1 to 4.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q2, Q3. Write a Python function for attribute closure and candidate keys.\n\n**Week 7: Normal forms and decomposition (Ch 4.3, 4.4).** Do: Assignment 2 Q4 to Q7. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 8: Transactions, ACID and anomalies (Ch 5.1, 5.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3. Reproduce a lost update and a non-repeatable read with two psql sessions.\n\n**Week 9: Serializability and locking (Ch 5.3 to 5.5).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2. Provoke and observe a deadlock in PostgreSQL.\n\n**Week 10: Recovery (Ch 6).** Do: Assignment 3 Q4. Read about write-ahead logging in the PostgreSQL documentation.\n\n**Week 11: Storage and indexing, B+ trees (Ch 7).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5, Q6. Create indexes and compare EXPLAIN ANALYZE output before and after.\n\n**Week 12: Query processing and optimisation (Ch 8).** Do: Assignment 3 Q7. Compare plans for equivalent queries (a join against a correlated subquery).\n\n**Week 13: Distributed databases, NoSQL and a design project (Ch 9).** Build a small application (Python plus PostgreSQL or SQLite): ER diagram, normalised schema, indexes, transactions, parameterised queries and a short report that justifies the design.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can write relational algebra expressions and translate them to SQL.\n- [ ] I can write joins, aggregates, subqueries, window functions and views correctly, and I understand NULL semantics.\n- [ ] I can map an ER diagram to tables with correct keys and constraints.\n- [ ] I can compute attribute closures and candidate keys and identify the normal form of a relation.\n- [ ] I can decompose a schema into 3NF and BCNF and check losslessness and dependency preservation.\n- [ ] I can test schedules for conflict-serializability and explain 2PL, deadlocks and isolation levels.\n- [ ] I can explain write-ahead logging and redo/undo recovery with a log.\n- [ ] I can compute B+ tree fan-out and height and compare access paths and join costs.\n- [ ] I can use a database safely from code (parameterised queries, transactions).\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Always run the query: predict the result by hand, then execute it and explain any difference.\n2. Design with data in mind: write five sample rows before deciding keys and constraints.\n3. Normalise by computing closures, not by intuition; then denormalise only deliberately.\n4. Use EXPLAIN before and after adding an index; never guess about performance.\n5. Treat every user input as hostile: parameterised queries always.\n","test":"# Database Systems: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Section B uses the shop database below.\n\n**Shop database**\n- customer(cid, name, city): (1, Adwoa, Accra), (2, Bekoe, Kumasi), (3, Cobbina, Accra), (4, Dede, Tamale), (5, Efua, Kumasi).\n- product(pid, pname, price, category): (10, Keyboard, 25.0, Accessory), (11, Mouse, 12.5, Accessory), (12, Monitor, 140.0, Display), (13, Laptop, 620.0, Computer), (14, Cable, 4.0, Accessory).\n- orders(oid, cid, odate): (100, 1, 2025-01-05), (101, 1, 2025-02-10), (102, 2, 2025-02-11), (103, 3, 2025-03-01), (104, 2, 2025-03-15), (105, 5, 2025-03-20).\n- item(oid, pid, qty), primary key (oid, pid): (100, 10, 2), (100, 11, 1), (101, 13, 1), (102, 12, 2), (102, 14, 5), (103, 10, 1), (103, 12, 1), (104, 11, 4), (105, 13, 1), (105, 14, 3).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Which of the following is NOT an ACID property?\nA) Atomicity  B) Consistency  C) Isolation  D) Scalability\n\n**2.** A foreign key\nA) must be unique  B) refers to a key of the same or another relation  C) may never be NULL  D) is always the primary key\n\n**3.** Which clause filters groups after aggregation?\nA) WHERE  B) HAVING  C) GROUP BY  D) ORDER BY\n\n**4.** For a column with values 4, NULL, 6, the value of COUNT(col) is\nA) 3  B) 0  C) 2  D) 1\n\n**5.** A LEFT OUTER JOIN of A and B returns\nA) all rows of A, with NULLs where B has no match  B) only matching rows  C) all rows of B  D) the Cartesian product\n\n**6.** In SQL, the expression NULL = NULL evaluates to\nA) TRUE  B) FALSE  C) UNKNOWN  D) 0\n\n**7.** A primary key\nA) may contain NULLs  B) uniquely identifies a row and cannot be NULL  C) must be a single column  D) must be a number\n\n**8.** A relation in which every non-trivial determinant is a superkey is in\nA) 1NF  B) 2NF  C) 3NF  D) BCNF\n\n**9.** From A → B and B → C we can derive A → C by\nA) transitivity  B) augmentation  C) reflexivity  D) decomposition\n\n**10.** If the closure X+ contains every attribute of R, then X is\nA) a foreign key  B) a minimal key necessarily  C) a superkey  D) a trivial dependency\n\n**11.** A 2NF violation arises from\nA) a transitive dependency  B) a partial dependency on part of a key  C) a multivalued column  D) a missing index\n\n**12.** A decomposition of R into R1 and R2 is lossless if R1 ∩ R2 is\nA) empty  B) a foreign key  C) a candidate key of R  D) a key of R1 or of R2\n\n**13.** A schedule is conflict-serializable if and only if its precedence graph is\nA) acyclic  B) a tree  C) complete  D) empty\n\n**14.** Strict two-phase locking holds exclusive locks\nA) only while writing  B) until the first read  C) until the transaction commits or aborts  D) never\n\n**15.** A deadlock is detected by finding\nA) a long wait  B) a cycle in the wait-for graph  C) an exclusive lock  D) an aborted transaction\n\n**16.** Which standard isolation level prevents phantoms?\nA) READ UNCOMMITTED  B) READ COMMITTED  C) REPEATABLE READ  D) SERIALIZABLE\n\n**17.** The write-ahead logging rule requires that\nA) the log record reaches disk before the changed data page  B) data pages are written before the log  C) the log is never written  D) all pages are written at commit\n\n**18.** The leaves of a B+ tree are\nA) not connected  B) linked in key order for range scans  C) always hashed  D) stored in memory only\n\n**19.** A hash index efficiently supports\nA) range queries  B) ordering  C) equality lookups  D) prefix matching\n\n**20.** In a block nested-loop join the outer relation should be\nA) the larger one  B) chosen at random  C) the one with an index  D) the smaller one\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. SQL (10 marks)** Write SQL queries (and give their results on the shop data) for: (a) each customer's number of orders, including customers with none, sorted by count descending and then name [3]; (b) the total value of each order (quantity × price) [3]; (c) the names of customers who have never placed an order, using NOT EXISTS [2]; (d) the revenue per city [2].\n\n**B2. Design theory (10 marks)** R(A, B, C, D, E) with F = {AB → C, C → D, D → E}. (a) Compute C+ and AB+ [2]. (b) Find all candidate keys [2]. (c) State the highest normal form of R and name the violating dependency [3]. (d) Give a lossless, dependency-preserving 3NF decomposition [3].\n\n**B3. Transactions (10 marks)** (a) For the schedule r1(X) r2(X) w2(X) r3(Y) w1(Y) w3(Y), list the conflicts, draw the precedence graph and decide whether it is conflict-serializable [4]. (b) Define the lost update anomaly using two transactions that both add 10 to a balance of 100 and show how the schedule produces 110 instead of 120 [3]. (c) A log has <T1 start>, <T1, A, 5, 9>, <T2 start>, <T2, B, 3, 7>, <T1 commit>, then a crash. State the redo and undo actions [3].\n\n**B4. Storage and query cost (10 marks)** (a) Block 4096 bytes, key 8 bytes, pointer 8 bytes: find the B+ tree order n and the minimum and maximum height of an index on 1,000,000 keys [4]. (b) The table has 100-byte records: how many blocks does a full scan read? [2] (c) R has 200 blocks, S has 40 blocks and M = 12 buffer blocks: the cost of a block nested-loop join with each as the outer [4].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **D** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **C** · 5 **A** · 6 **C** · 7 **B** · 8 **D** · 9 **A** · 10 **C** · 11 **B** · 12 **D** · 13 **A** · 14 **C** · 15 **B** · 16 **D** · 17 **A** · 18 **B** · 19 **C** · 20 **D**\n\nBrief reasons: 4. COUNT(col) ignores the NULL. 6. Any comparison with NULL is UNKNOWN. 12. The lossless-join condition: (R1 ∩ R2) → R1 or (R1 ∩ R2) → R2. 16. The standard allows phantoms at REPEATABLE READ. 20. The cost is b_outer + ceil(b_outer/(M - 2)) × b_inner, minimised with the smaller outer.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a)\n```sql\nSELECT c.name, COUNT(o.oid) AS n\nFROM customer c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.cid = c.cid\nGROUP BY c.cid ORDER BY n DESC, c.name;\n```\nStep 1: orders per customer (the left join keeps Dede with none): Adwoa 2 (orders 100, 101), Bekoe 2 (102, 104), Cobbina 1 (103), Efua 1 (105), Dede 0.\nStep 2: ORDER BY n DESC, name.\nResult: Adwoa 2, Bekoe 2, Cobbina 1, Efua 1, Dede 0.\n(b)\n```sql\nSELECT o.oid, SUM(i.qty * p.price) AS total\nFROM orders o JOIN item i ON i.oid = o.oid JOIN product p ON p.pid = i.pid\nGROUP BY o.oid ORDER BY o.oid;\n```\nStep 1: multiply qty by price on every item row and sum per order.\nResult: 100 → 62.5 (2 × 25 + 12.5); 101 → 620; 102 → 300 (280 + 20); 103 → 165 (25 + 140); 104 → 50; 105 → 632 (620 + 12).\n(c)\n```sql\nSELECT name FROM customer c\nWHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.cid = c.cid);\n```\nResult: Dede.\n(d)\n```sql\nSELECT c.city, SUM(i.qty * p.price) AS rev\nFROM customer c JOIN orders o ON o.cid = c.cid\n     JOIN item i ON i.oid = o.oid JOIN product p ON p.pid = i.pid\nGROUP BY c.city ORDER BY rev DESC;\n```\nStep 1: assign each order total to its customer's city; Accra customers have orders 100 (62.5), 101 (620) and 103 (165); Kumasi customers have orders 102 (300), 104 (50) and 105 (632).\nStep 2: Accra = 62.5 + 620 + 165 = 847.5; Kumasi = 300 + 50 + 632 = 982.0.\nResult: Kumasi 982.0 (300 + 50 + 632), Accra 847.5 (62.5 + 620 + 165).\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Step 1: C+: start {C}; C → D adds D; D → E adds E: **{C, D, E}**.\nStep 2: AB+: start {A, B}; AB → C adds C; C → D adds D; D → E adds E: **{A, B, C, D, E}**.\n(b) A and B never occur on a right-hand side, so both are in every key; AB+ = R, so **AB is the only candidate key**.\n(c) C → D has C as a non-superkey (C+ = CDE) and D is not prime, so 3NF is violated (this is a transitive dependency of D on the key through C). There are no partial dependencies because no non-prime attribute depends on A alone or on B alone (A+ = A, B+ = B), so R is in **2NF**.\n(d) Synthesis: the minimal cover is F. Relations (A, B, C), (C, D), (D, E). (A, B, C) contains the key AB, so nothing more is needed. This decomposition is **lossless** and **dependency-preserving**, and each relation is in BCNF.\n\n**B3.**\n(a) T1: r1(X) w1(Y); T2: r2(X) w2(X); T3: r3(Y) w3(Y). Conflicts: r1(X) before w2(X): T1 → T2. r3(Y) before w1(Y): T3 → T1. w1(Y) before w3(Y): T1 → T3. r2(X) and r1(X) are both reads (no conflict). The edges T3 → T1 and T1 → T3 form a cycle, so the schedule is **not conflict-serializable**.\n(b) Balance = 100. T1 reads 100; T2 reads 100; T1 writes 110; T2 writes 110 (it computed from its stale read). The final balance is **110**; the serial result would be 120. The update of T1 was lost.\n(c) T1 is committed: **redo A = 9**. T2 is not committed: **undo B back to 3**.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Step 1: a node has n pointers and n - 1 keys: 8n + 8 (n - 1) ≤ 4096, so 16n ≤ 4104 and n ≤ 256.5: **n = 256** (up to 255 keys per node; the minimum is 128 children for internal nodes and 128 keys per leaf).\nStep 2 (best case, nodes full): leaves = ⌈10^6/255⌉ = 3922; above them ⌈3922/256⌉ = 16; then the root: **height 3**.\nStep 3 (worst case, nodes half full): leaves = ⌈10^6/128⌉ = 7813; above them ⌈7813/128⌉ = 62; then the root: **height 3** (the same).\nA lookup reads 3 index blocks plus 1 data block.\n(b) Step 1: records per block = ⌊4096/100⌋ = 40.\nStep 2: blocks = 1,000,000/40 = **25,000 blocks**.\n(c) Cost = b_outer + ⌈b_outer/(M - 2)⌉ × b_inner with M - 2 = 10 blocks per chunk.\nStep 1: S as the outer: 40 + ⌈40/10⌉ × 200 = 40 + 4 × 200 = **840**.\nStep 2: R as the outer: 200 + ⌈200/10⌉ × 40 = 200 + 20 × 40 = **1000**. The smaller outer relation is cheaper.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT an ACID property?","options":["Atomicity","Consistency","Isolation","Scalability"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A foreign key","options":["must be unique","refers to a key of the same or another relation","may never be NULL","is always the primary key"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which clause filters groups after aggregation?","options":["WHERE","HAVING","GROUP BY","ORDER BY"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"For a column with values 4, NULL, 6, the value of COUNT(col) is","options":["3","0","2","1"],"answer":2,"explain":"COUNT(col) ignores the NULL.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A LEFT OUTER JOIN of A and B returns","options":["all rows of A, with NULLs where B has no match","only matching rows","all rows of B","the Cartesian product"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In SQL, the expression NULL = NULL evaluates to","options":["TRUE","FALSE","UNKNOWN","0"],"answer":2,"explain":"Any comparison with NULL is UNKNOWN.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A primary key","options":["may contain NULLs","uniquely identifies a row and cannot be NULL","must be a single column","must be a number"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A relation in which every non-trivial determinant is a superkey is in","options":["1NF","2NF","3NF","BCNF"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"From A → B and B → C we can derive A → C by","options":["transitivity","augmentation","reflexivity","decomposition"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"If the closure X+ contains every attribute of R, then X is","options":["a foreign key","a minimal key necessarily","a superkey","a trivial dependency"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A 2NF violation arises from","options":["a transitive dependency","a partial dependency on part of a key","a multivalued column","a missing index"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A decomposition of R into R1 and R2 is lossless if R1 ∩ R2 is","options":["empty","a foreign key","a candidate key of R","a key of R1 or of R2"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lossless-join condition: (R1 ∩ R2) → R1 or (R1 ∩ R2) → R2.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A schedule is conflict-serializable if and only if its precedence graph is","options":["acyclic","a tree","complete","empty"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Strict two-phase locking holds exclusive locks","options":["only while writing","until the first read","until the transaction commits or aborts","never"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A deadlock is detected by finding","options":["a long wait","a cycle in the wait-for graph","an exclusive lock","an aborted transaction"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which standard isolation level prevents phantoms?","options":["READ UNCOMMITTED","READ COMMITTED","REPEATABLE READ","SERIALIZABLE"],"answer":3,"explain":"The standard allows phantoms at REPEATABLE READ.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The write-ahead logging rule requires that","options":["the log record reaches disk before the changed data page","data pages are written before the log","the log is never written","all pages are written at commit"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The leaves of a B+ tree are","options":["not connected","linked in key order for range scans","always hashed","stored in memory only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A hash index efficiently supports","options":["range queries","ordering","equality lookups","prefix matching"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a block nested-loop join the outer relation should be","options":["the larger one","chosen at random","the one with an index","the smaller one"],"answer":3,"explain":"The cost is b_outer + ceil(b_outer/(M - 2)) × b_inner, minimised with the smaller outer.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Physical data independence means we can change","options":["the queries without changing the schema","the storage structures and indexes without changing the application programs","the schema without changing the data","the users without changing the data"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The relational algebra operator that chooses rows satisfying a condition is","options":["projection π","selection σ","join ⋈","rename ρ"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"If R has 5 tuples and S has 7 tuples, R × S has","options":["12 tuples","7 tuples","35 tuples","5 tuples"],"answer":2,"explain":"5 × 7 = 35.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A FULL OUTER JOIN keeps","options":["only matching rows","unmatched rows of the left table only","unmatched rows of the right table only","unmatched rows of both tables"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which SELECT clause is evaluated first?","options":["FROM","SELECT","ORDER BY","HAVING"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The aggregates SUM and AVG","options":["treat NULL as zero","fail when a NULL is present","ignore NULL values","return NULL if any value is NULL"],"answer":2,"explain":"They skip NULLs.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"If the subquery of `x NOT IN (subquery)` returns a NULL, then for a non-matching x the result is","options":["TRUE","never TRUE (UNKNOWN)","FALSE","an error"],"answer":1,"explain":"NOT IN with a NULL gives UNKNOWN for non-members.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A window function such as RANK() OVER (...)","options":["is the same as GROUP BY","removes duplicates","can be used only in WHERE","computes a value per row without collapsing rows"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A weak entity","options":["has no primary key of its own and depends on an owner entity","never has attributes","is always in a 1:1 relationship","has a multivalued key"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A many-to-many relationship is mapped to","options":["a foreign key on one side","a multivalued attribute","a separate table containing both keys","a view"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT one of Armstrong's axioms?","options":["reflexivity","augmentation","transitivity","idempotence"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Every relation in BCNF is also in","options":["1NF only","3NF","none of the other normal forms","4NF necessarily"],"answer":1,"explain":"BCNF is stricter than 3NF.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A minimal cover requires that every right-hand side","options":["is a single attribute","is a key","is empty","is the whole relation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The BCNF decomposition algorithm always gives","options":["dependency preservation","3NF only","a lossless join, but it may lose dependencies","a smaller number of relations than 3NF synthesis"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"T1 reads a value written by T2 which later aborts. This anomaly is","options":["a dirty read","a phantom","a lost update","write skew"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A cascadeless schedule is one in which","options":["transactions never conflict","transactions read only data written by committed transactions","all transactions are serial","no deadlock occurs"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In the wait-die scheme, if an older transaction requests a lock held by a younger one, the older transaction","options":["aborts","kills the younger transaction","is granted the lock at once","waits"],"answer":3,"explain":"An older transaction waits for a younger one; a younger one requesting from an older one dies.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In ARIES, the redo pass","options":["undoes uncommitted work","deletes the log","repeats history from the log","forces all pages"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a B+ tree of order n = 10, a non-root internal node has at least how many children?","options":["3","5","7","10"],"answer":1,"explain":"ceil(10/2) = 5.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"According to the CAP theorem, during a network partition a distributed system must choose between","options":["consistency and availability","speed and cost","atomicity and durability","reads and writes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
